{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 76 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "9cda98b6c185f890e4ee4de37de27cca50c21ef1d5d48a60ee4d74e2f1d92b60" :edb/db-before-t 75 :edb/db-after-t 76 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533388 50 #inst "2026-10-10T07:43:51.514Z" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186054563 1071 "Find the solution of $x^2-3x \\lt  0.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186054563 1071 "Find the solution of $x^2-3x \\lt 0$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186054563 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x & <0 \\\\ x(x-3) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x-3)=0.$ The roots are $x=0$ and $x=3,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty,0),$ $(0,3),$ and $(3,\\infty).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x(x-3)$ | $({\\color{red}-})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{blue}+}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{red}-}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{blue}+})={\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x-3) \\lt 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x-3)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(0, 3).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x^2-3x \\lt 0$ for all $x\\in (0,3).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186054563 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} - 3x & < 0 \\\\ x(x - 3) & < 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x-3)=0$. The roots are $x=0$ and $x=3$, and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty,0)$, $(0,3)$, and $(3,\\infty)$.\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x(x-3)$ | $({\\color{red}-})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{blue}+}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{red}-}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{blue}+})={\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x-3) \\lt 0$, we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x-3)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(0, 3)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $x^2-3x \\lt 0$ for all $x\\in (0,3)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186054564 1071 "Find the solution of $x^2-3x +2\\geq 0$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186054564 1071 "Find the solution of $x^2-3x +2\\geq 0.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186054564 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x+2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x-1)(x-2)=0.$ The roots are $x = 1$ and $x=2,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty,1),$ $(1,2),$ and $(2,\\infty).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $(1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x-1)(x-2)$ | $({\\color{red}-})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{blue}+}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{red}-}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{blue}+})={\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x-1)(x-2) \\geq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-1)(x-2)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty, 1)$ and $(2,\\infty).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $(x-1)(x-2) \\geq 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty, 1] \\cup [2, \\infty).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186054564 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} - 3x + 2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x - 1)(x - 2) & \\ge 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x-2)=0\n$$\n\nThe roots are $x = 1$ and $x=2$, and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty,1)$, $(1,2)$, and $(2,\\infty)$.\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,1)$ | $(1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x-1)(x-2)$ | $({\\color{red}-})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{blue}+}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{red}-})={\\color{red}-}$ | $({\\color{blue}+})\\cdot({\\color{blue}+})={\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x-2) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-1)(x-2)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty, 1)$ and $(2,\\infty)$. Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=1$ and $x=2$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x-2) \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in (-\\infty, 1] \\cup [2, \\infty)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186054565 1071 "Find the solution of $x^2+x \\leq 6$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186054565 1071 "Find the solution of $x^2+x \\leq 6.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186054565 1074 "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x & \\le 6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ (x-2)(x+3) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x-2)(x+3) = 0.$ The roots are $x=2$ and $x=-3,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty, -3),$ $(-3,2),$ and $(2,\\infty).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-3)$ | $(-3,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x+3)(x-2)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x-2)(x+3) \\leq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-2)(x+3)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-3,2).$ Also, we know that the expression equals zero at the points $x=-3$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $(x-2)(x+3) \\leq 0$ for all $x\\in[-3,2].$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186054565 1074 "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + x & \\le 6 \\\\ x^{2} + x - 6 & \\le 0 \\\\ (x - 2)(x + 3) & \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+3) = 0\n$$\n\nThe roots are $x=2$ and $x=-3$, and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty, -3)$, $(-3,2)$, and $(2,\\infty)$.\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-3)$ | $(-3,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x+3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x+3)(x-2)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+3) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nwe look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-2)(x+3)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-3,2)$. Also, we know that the expression equals zero at the points $x=-3$ and $x=2$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+3) \\leq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in[-3,2]$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186054566 1071 "Complete the following sign table:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ |  |  |  |\n| $x+1$ |  |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ |  |  |  |" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186054566 1071 "Complete the following sign table:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ |  |  |  |\n| $x+1$ |  |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ |  |  |  |" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186054566 1074 "We need to fill in each box with the sign of the corresponding expression on the corresponding interval.\n\nLet's start down the first column. In the interval $(-\\infty, -1)$, we choose a test point $x_1=-2$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2 &= -2 - 2 =-4 < 0, \\\\\nx + 1 &= -2 + 1 =-1 < 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso both the expressions $x-2$ and $x+1$ are negative $({\\color{red}-})$ on the interval $(-\\infty,-1)$.\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ |  |  |  |\n\nTo find the sign of $(x-2)(x+1)$ on this interval, we can just multiply the corresponding signs of $x-2$ and $x+1$:\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}-}) \\times ({\\color{red}-}) = ({\\color{blue}+})\n$$\n\nSo $(x-2)(x+1)$ is positive $({\\color{blue}+})$ on this interval, and we can complete the first column of the table:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |  |  |\n\nTo find the entries in the other columns, we use the same procedure.\n\n- In the interval $(-1,2)$, we can choose a test point $x_2=0$. Then we have\n$x - 2|= 0 - 2 = (-),; x + 1|= 0 + 1 = (+),; (x - 2)(x + 1)|= (-) \\cdot (+) = (-)$,\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |\n\n- In the interval $(2,\\infty)$, we can choose a test point $x_3=3$. Then we have\n$x - 2|= 3 - 2 = (+),; x + 1|= 3 + 1 = (+),; (x - 2)(x + 1)|= (+) \\cdot (+) = (+)$,\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186054566 1074 "We need to fill in each box with the sign of the corresponding expression on the corresponding interval.\n\nLet's start down the first column. In the interval $(-\\infty, -1),$ we choose a test point $x_1=-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =-2-2=-4<0, \\\\ x+1 & =-2+1=-1<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso both the expressions $x-2$ and $x+1$ are negative $({\\color{red}-})$ on the interval $(-\\infty,-1).$\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ |  |  |  |\n\nTo find the sign of $(x-2)(x+1)$ on this interval, we can just multiply the corresponding signs of $x-2$ and $x+1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}-}) \\times ({\\color{red}-}) = ({\\color{blue}+})\n$$\n\nSo $(x-2)(x+1)$ is positive $({\\color{blue}+})$ on this interval, and we can complete the first column of the table:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |  |  |\n\nTo find the entries in the other columns, we use the same procedure.\n\n- In the interval $(-1,2),$ we can choose a test point $x_2=0.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =0-2=(-), \\\\ x+1 & =0+1=(+), \\\\ (x-2)(x+1) & =(-)\\times (+)=(-),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |  |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |  |\n\n- In the interval $(2,\\infty),$ we can choose a test point $x_3=3.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =3-2=(+), \\\\ x+1 & =3+1=(+), \\\\ (x-2)(x+1) & =(+)\\times (+)=(+),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| $(x-2)(x+1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186059592 1074 "The coordinates of the point are $(-2,3)$.\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=-2$, and\n- the imaginary part is $y=3$.\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is\n\n$$\nz=-2+3\\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186059592 1074 "The coordinates of the point are $(-2,3)$.\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=-2,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is $z=-2+3\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186059593 1074 "The coordinates of the point are $(-4,0)$.\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=-4$, and\n- the imaginary part is $y=0$.\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is\n\n$$\nz=-4+0\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to $z= -4$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186059593 1074 "The coordinates of the point are $(-4,0).$\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=-4,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is $z=-4+0\\textrm{i},$ which can be simplified to $z= -4.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186059594 1074 "We are given the complex number\n\n$$\nz=4+\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\n.\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=4$, and\n- the imaginary part is $y=1$.\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the point are $(4,1)$, as shown in the Argand diagram below.\n\n![](images/3e/3ec41e707260b15e36da91542b98a66abcaa7950a3d4b06a95f919a204aec797.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186059594 1074 "We are given the complex number $z=4+\\textrm{i}$.\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=4,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the point are $(4,1)$, as shown in the Argand diagram below.\n\n![](images/3e/3ec41e707260b15e36da91542b98a66abcaa7950a3d4b06a95f919a204aec797.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045