textrm{i}}{1 + 2 \\textrm{i}}$, calculate $p$ and $q$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186059606 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $1+2\\textrm{i}$ to get rid of the fraction and isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 - 3\\textrm{i} &= \\frac{p - 2q\\textrm{i}}{1 + 2\\textrm{i}} \\\\\n(2 - 3\\textrm{i})(1 + 2\\textrm{i}) &= p - 2q\\textrm{i} \\\\\n2 + 4\\textrm{i} - 3\\textrm{i} - 6\\textrm{i}^{2} &= p - 2q\\textrm{i} \\\\\n2 + \\textrm{i} - 6(-1) &= p - 2q\\textrm{i} \\\\\n8 + \\textrm{i} &= p - 2q\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get\n$8 = p$.\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get\n$1|=-2q; q|=-\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, $p=8$ and\n\n$$\nq = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186059606 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $1+2\\textrm{i}$ to get rid of the fraction and isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\text{i} & =\\frac{p-2q\\text{i}}{1+2\\text{i}} \\\\ (2-3\\text{i})(1+2\\text{i}) & =p-2q\\text{i} \\\\ 2+4\\text{i}-3\\text{i}-6{\\text{i}}^{2} & =p-2q\\text{i} \\\\ 2+\\text{i}-6(-1) & =p-2q\\text{i} \\\\ 8+\\text{i} & =p-2q\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get\n\n$$\n8 = p.\n$$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-2q \\\\ q & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=8$ and $q = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186059607 1071 "Given that $3+5\\textrm{i} = (p+\\textrm{i}q)(1+\\textrm{i})$, calculate $p$ and $q$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186059607 1071 "Given that $3+5\\textrm{i} = (p+\\textrm{i}q)(1+\\textrm{i}),$ calculate $p$ and $q.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186059607 1074 "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $1+\\textrm{i}$ to isolate t