}\\cos 2x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186064106 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x \\cos{2x} \\textrm{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186064107 1071 "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5\\, \\textrm{d}x}{\\mid 5x \\mid \\sqrt{(5x)^2-1}} =}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186064107 1071 "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5\\, \\textrm{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^2-1}}  = }$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186064107 1074 "This resembles the basic integral for $\\textrm{arcsec}$,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\mid u \\mid \\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec}(u)+C\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int(5dx)/(\\begin{vmatrix}5x & \\sqrt{(5x)^{2} - 1}) &= \\int\\frac{du}{\\mid u\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{u^{2} - 1}} \\\\\n&= arcsec(u) + C \\\\\n&= arcsec(5x) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186064107 1074 "This resembles the basic integral for $\\textrm{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\,\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}} & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186064110 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=3x^2+1$ where the tangent line has a slope of $-1$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186064110 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=3x^2+1$ where the tangent line has a slope of $-1.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186064110 1074 "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=3x^2+1\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to $f'(x)$. Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(3x^{2} + 1) \\\\\n&= 3\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(1) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2x + 0 \\\\\n&= 6x + 0 \\\\\n&= 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set $f'(x) = -1$ and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= -1 \\\\\n6x &= -1 \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{1}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent is equal to $-1$ at the point where\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac 1 6\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186064110 1074 "Let $f(x)=3x^2+1.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to $f'(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =6x+0 \\\\  & =6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set $f'(x) = -1$ and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-1 \\\\ 6x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent is equal to $-1$ at the point where $x=-\\dfrac 1 6.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186064116 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 2t+3t^2$, where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=1$ when $t=0$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186064116 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 2t+3t^2,$ where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=1$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186064116 1074 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= \\intv(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int(2t + 3t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= t^{2} + t^{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(0) = 1$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= (0)^{2} + (0)^{3} + C \\\\\nC &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^3+t^2+1\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186064116 1074 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(0) = 1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(0)^{2}+(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^3+t^2+1.\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186064117 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = 1-2t$, where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=1$ and velocity $v=2$ when $t=1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t >0$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186064117 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = 1-2t,$ where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=1$ and velocity $v=2$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t >0.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186064117 1074 "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\inta(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int(1 - 2t)dt \\\\\n&= t - t^{2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) = 2$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= (1) - (1)^{2} + C \\\\\nC &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by\n\n$$\nv(t) = t-t^2 + 2\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= \\intv(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int(t - t^{2} + 2)dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}t^{2} - \\frac{1}{3}t^{3} + 2t + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(1) = 1$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} - \\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3} + 2(1) + K \\\\\nK &= -\\frac{7}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{1}{2}t^2-\\dfrac{1}{3}t^3 +2t -\\dfrac{7}{6}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186064117 1074 "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (1-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t-{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =(1)-(1)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n