nd is missing the $x^3$ term. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing term with a coefficient of zero, as follows:\n\n$$\n2x^4-7x^2-x+8 = 2x^4+{\\color{red}0}x^3-7x^2-x+8\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nx^{2} - 12x^{2} - 5; x^{2} - 12x^{4} + 0x^{3} - 7x^{2} - 0x + 8; x^{2} - 12x^{4} + 0x^{3} - 2x^{2}_(―); x^{2} - 12x^{4} + 0x^{3} - 5x^{2} - 0x + 8; x^{2} - 12x^{4} + 0x^{3}- 5x^{2} + 0x + 5_(―); x^{2} - 12x^{4} + 0x^{3} - 5x^{2}- 0x + 3\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n- the quotient is ${2x^2 - 5}$, and\n- the remainder is ${-x+3}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^4 - 7x^2 - x +8}{x^2 - 1} = 2x^2-5 + \\dfrac{-x+3}{x^2-1}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^4 - 7x^2 - x +8}{x^2 - 1} = 2x^2-5 - \\dfrac{x-3}{x^2-1}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068607 1071 "Find the points of intersection, if any, of the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1$ and the line $y=-x+3$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068607 1071 "Find the points of intersection, if any, of the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1$ and the line $y=-x+3.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186068607 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x-2}+1 \\\\ y=-x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-2}+1 & =-x+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-2} & =-x+2 \\\\ 1 & =(-x+2)(x-2) \\\\ 1 & =-{x}^{2}+4x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{16-20}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-x+3$ does not intersect the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30eb7faffa9cdfbffedbbf0c9d48408fb1be1b8101da80864c4bced5d53d9903.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186068607 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = \\frac{1}{x - 2} + 1; y =-x + 3\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{x - 2} + 1 &= -x + 3 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{x - 2} &= -x + 2 \\\\\n1 &= (-x + 2)(x - 2) \\\\\n1 &= -x^{2} + 4x - 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x + 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (4 ± \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} - 4(1)(5)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{16 - 20}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{-4}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line\n\n$$\ny=-x+3\n$$\n\ndoes not intersect the reciprocal function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30eb7faffa9cdfbffedbbf0c9d48408fb1be1b8101da80864c4bced5d53d9903.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068608 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x-3 \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and then solving for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\ (x-2)(2x-3) & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-7x+6 & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-7x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(2x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=1$ and $x=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- First, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\  & =2(1)-3 \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(1,-1).$\n\n- Then, substituting $x=\\dfrac{5}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5}{2})-3 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $\\left( \\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,-1)$ and $\\left( \\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186068608 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = 2x - 3; y = \\frac{1}{x - 2}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and then solving for $x$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 3 &= \\frac{1}{x - 2} \\\\\n(x - 2)(2x - 3) &= 1 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 7x + 6 &= 1 \\\\\n2x^{2} - 7x + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(2x - 5) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 1, \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=1$ and\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n- First, substituting $x=1$, we get\n$y|= 2x - 3; = 2(1) - 3; =-1$,\nso one point of intersection is $(1,-1)$.\n- Then, substituting $x=\\dfrac{5}{2}$, we get\n$y|= 2x - 3; = 2(\\frac{5}{2}) - 3; = 2$,\nso the other point of intersection is $\\left(\\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,-1)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{5}{2}, 2 \\right)\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068612 1071 "The line $y=2x+3$ does not intersect the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{k}{x+3}$, where $k \\neq 0$. What is the range of possible values of $k$?" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068612 1071 "The line $y=2x+3$ does not intersect the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{k}{x+3}$, where $k \\neq 0$. What is the range of possible values of $k?$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186068612 1074 "To determine whether the line\n\n$$\ny=2x+3\n$$\n\nintersects the reciprocal function\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{k}{x+3}\n$$\n\nwe need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{y = \\frac{k}{x + 3}; y = 2x + 3\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{k}{x + 3} &= 2x + 3 \\\\\nk &= (2x + 3)(x + 3) \\\\\nk &= 2x^{2} + 9x + 9 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 9x + 9 - k &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (-9 ± \\sqrt{9^{2} - 4(2)(9 - k)})/(2(2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-9 ± \\sqrt{81 - 72 + 8k}}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-9 ± \\sqrt{9 + 8k}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the line *not* to intersect the reciprocal function, the quadratic formula above must *not* give any real solutions.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 + 8k & < 0 \\\\ 8k & < - 9 \\\\ k & < - \\frac{9}{8}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is\n\n$$\nk<-\\dfrac{9}{8}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068612 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=2x+3$ intersects the reciprocal function $y=\\dfrac{k}{x+3},$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{k}{x+3} \\\\ y=2x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{x+3} & =2x+3 \\\\ k & =(2x+3)(x+3) \\\\ k & =2{x}^{2}+9x+9 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+9x+9-k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{{9}^{2}-4(2)(9-k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{81-72+8k}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{9+8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the line *not* to intersect the reciprocal function, the quadratic formula above must *not* give any real solutions.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9+8k & <0 \\\\ 8k & <-9 \\\\ k & <-\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<-\\dfrac{9}{8}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186068614 1071 "Write down an integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=\\sqrt x$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b98474e6de2c94876a75758d56a7fb356de22bccd029bfeed0207affebab09ce.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068614 1071 "Write down an integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=\\sqrt x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b98474e6de2c94876a75758d56a7fb356de22bccd029bfeed0207affebab09ce.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186068614 1074 "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=0$ and $x=1$. So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/1b/1b1c2b1826fa988ba23f7270b4c8bbdc91bf03066e8ca3638def329466d5b93e.png)\n\nThe vertical line\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt x$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{0}^{1}[(upper function) - (lower function)]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[(\\sqrt{x}) - (x^{2})]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{x} - x^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068614 1074 "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=0$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/1b/1b1c2b1826fa988ba23f7270b4c8bbdc91bf03066e8ca3638def329466d5b93e.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\sqrt{x})-({x}^{2})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{x}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186068615 1071 "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $f(x) =x^2-2x+2$ and $g(x) = 2$, shown below.\n\n![](images/01/01f139c53b6328341b463f5ad941d37fccb241fff109c0584207017341e8e5f7.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068615 1071 "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $f(x) =x^2-2x+2$ and $g(x) = 2,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/01/01f139c53b6328341b463f5ad941d37fccb241fff109c0584207017341e8e5f7.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186068615 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 2 &= 2 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2$.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/39/39798ef4ff6e898527864628dc2750f88b115c03b2d6b8fb729f30b21d2d2bf5.png)\n\nThe vertical line\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2-2x+2$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2$.\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{0}^{2}[(upper function) - (lower function)]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}[(2) - (x^{2} - 2x + 2)]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}(-x^{2} + 2x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}(2x - x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\left.(x^{2} - \\frac{1}{3}x^{3})\\right|_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (2^{2} - \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 2^{3}) - (0 - 0) \\\\\n&= (4 - \\frac{8}{3}) - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068615 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+2 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/39/39798ef4ff6e898527864628dc2750f88b115c03b2d6b8fb729f30b21d2d2bf5.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2-2x+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(2)-({x}^{2}-2x+2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3})-(0-0) \\\\  & =(4-\\frac{8}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186068616 1071 "What is the integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=x^2+x+5$ and $y=x+9$?" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068616 1071 "What is the integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=x^2+x+5$ and $y=x+9?$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186068616 1074 "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](images/5f/5f6980e3a7e4c7d1a5cc5d9b93a0110764b34ea6b7a4bec7c828f04cef842f7f.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + x + 5 &= x + 9 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 2)(x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2$.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/3c/3c52f1405079380459e60ad35e05218d1f0468ee177f1e383498c672d48eaa8d.png)\n\nThe vertical line\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2+x+5$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+9$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{-2}^{2}[(upper function) - (lower function)]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-2}^{2}[(x + 9) - (x^{2} + x + 5)]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{-2}^{2}(4 - x^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068616 1074 "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](images/5f/5f6980e3a7e4c7d1a5cc5d9b93a0110764b34ea6b7a4bec7c828f04cef842f7f.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x+5 & =x+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/3c/3c52f1405079380459e60ad35e05218d1f0468ee177f1e383498c672d48eaa8d.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2+x+5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+9.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(x+9)-({x}^{2}+x+5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186068620 1071 "Solve for $a$ where $\\dfrac{2a -5}{3a^2} + \\dfrac{1}{a} = \\dfrac{a-1}{2a^2}$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068620 1071 "Solve for $a$ where $\\dfrac{2a -5}{3a^2} + \\dfrac{1}{a} = \\dfrac{a-1}{2a^2}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186068620 1074 "First, note that $a = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $3a^2$, $a$, and $2a^2$, so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(3a^2, a, 2a^2) = 6a^2\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6a^2$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2a - 5}{3a^{2}} + \\frac{1}{a} &= \\frac{a - 1}{2a^{2}} \\\\\n6a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{2a - 5}{3a^{2}} + \\frac{1}{a}) &= 6a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{a - 1}{2a^{2}}) \\\\\n6a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{2a - 5}{3a^{2}}) + 6a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) &= 6a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{a - 1}{2a^{2}}) \\\\\n2 \\cdot 3a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{2a - 5}{3a^{2}}) + 6a \\cdot a \\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) &= 3 \\cdot 2a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{a - 1}{2a^{2}}) \\\\\n2 \\cdot 3a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{2a - 5}{3a^{2}}) + 6a \\cdot a \\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) &= 3 \\cdot 2a^{2} \\cdot (\\frac{a - 1}{2a^{2}}) \\\\\n2 \\cdot (2a - 5) + 6a \\cdot (1) &= 3 \\cdot (a - 1) \\\\\n2(2a - 5) + 6a &= 3(a - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a$, and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(2a - 5) + 6a &= 3(a - 1) \\\\\n4a - 10 + 6a &= 3a - 3 \\\\\n10a - 10 &= 3a - 3 \\\\\n10a - 3a &= -3 + 10 \\\\\n7a &= 7 \\\\\na &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. Since $a=1$ is different from $a=0$, we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a=1$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068620 1074 "First, note that $a = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $3a^2,$ $a,$ and $2a^2,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(3a^2, a, 2a^2) = 6a^2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6a^2$ and simplifying, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{a} & =\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}} \\\\ 6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{a}) & =6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}})+6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) & =6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot 3{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}})+6a\\cdot a\\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) & =3\\cdot 2{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot \\cancel{3{a}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{\\cancel{3{a}^{2}}})+6a\\cdot \\cancel{a}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{a}}) & =3\\cdot \\cancel{2{a}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{\\cancel{2{a}^{2}}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot (2a-5)+6a\\cdot (1) & =3\\cdot (a-1) \\\\ 2(2a-5)+6a & =3(a-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2a-5)+6a & =3(a-1) \\\\ 4a-10+6a & =3a-3 \\\\ 10a-10 & =3a-3 \\\\ 10a-3a & =-3+10 \\\\ 7a & =7 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. Since $a=1$ is different from $a=0,$ we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a=1.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186068621 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $3 - \\dfrac{10x+7}{8x}  = \\dfrac{6x-3}{2x}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186068621 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $3 - \\dfrac{10x+7}{8x} = \\dfrac{6x-3}{2x}$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068621 1074 "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $8x$ and $2x$, so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(8x,2x) = 8x\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $8x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 - \\frac{10x + 7}{8x} &= \\frac{6x - 3}{2x} \\\\\n8x \\cdot (3 - \\frac{10x + 7}{8x}) &= 8x \\cdot (\\frac{6x - 3}{2x}) \\\\\n8x \\cdot 3 - 8x \\cdot (\\frac{10x + 7}{8x}) &= 8x \\cdot (\\frac{6x - 3}{2x}) \\\\\n24x - 8x \\cdot (\\frac{10x + 7}{8x}) &= 4 \\cdot 2x \\cdot (\\frac{6x - 3}{2x}) \\\\\n24x - (10x + 7) &= 4 \\cdot (6x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x$, and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24x - (10x + 7) &= 4 \\cdot (6x - 3) \\\\\n14x - 7 &= 24x - 12 \\\\\n24x - 14x &= -7 + 12 \\\\\n10x &= 5 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since\n\n$$\nx= \\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nis different from $x=0$, we accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\nx= \\dfrac 12\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186068621 1074 "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $8x$ and $2x,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(8x,2x) = 8x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $8x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{10x+7}{8x} & =\\frac{6x-3}{2x} \\\\ 8x\\cdot (3-\\frac{10x+7}{8x}) & =8x\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{2x}) \\\\ 8x\\cdot 3-8x\\cdot (\\frac{10x+7}{8x}) & =8x\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{2x}) \\\\ 24x-\\cancel{8x}\\cdot (\\frac{10x+7}{\\cancel{8x}}) & =4\\cdot \\cancel{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{\\cancel{2x}}) \\\\ 24x-(10x+7) & =4\\cdot (6x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24x-(10x+7) & =4\\cdot (6x-3) \\\\ 14x-7 & =24x-12 \\\\ 24x-14x & =-7+12 \\\\ 10x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x= \\dfrac 12$ is different from $x=0,$ we accept it as a solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\dfrac 12.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186078330 1071 "For the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=2,\n$$\n\nuse the tangent line approximation at $x=1$ to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186078330 1071 "For the initial value problem\n\n$y' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=2$,\n\nuse the tangent line approximation at $x=1$ to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186078330 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= y(a) + y^{′}(a)(x - a) \\\\\n&= y(1) + y^{′}(1)(x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1) = 2$. We also know that\n\n$$\ny'(x) = 2xy\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\ny'(1) = 2(1)(2) = 4\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and $y'(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= 2 + (4)(x - 1) \\\\\n&= 2 + 4x - 4 \\\\\n&= 4x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)\n$$\n\nusing\n\n$$\nL\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right)\\approx L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right) = 4\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right) - 2 = 4\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186078330 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1) = 2.$ We also know that $y'(x) = 2xy,$ so\n\n$$\ny'(1) = 2(1)(2) = 4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and $y'(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+(4)(x-1) \\\\  & =2+4x-4 \\\\  & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)$ using $L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right).$ We get\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right)\\approx L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right) = 4\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right) - 2 = 4.\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186083007 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac { 8}{t^5}  \\,\\textrm{d}t.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186083007 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac {8}{t^5} \\,\\textrm{d}t$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186083007 1074 "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{2}^{∞}\\frac{8}{t^{5}}dt &= lim_(a → ∞)\\int_{2}^{a}\\frac{8}{t^{5}}dt \\\\\n&= 8lim_(a → ∞)\\int_{2}^{a}t^{-5}dt \\\\\n&= 8lim_(a → ∞)[- \\frac{1}{4}t^{-4}]_{2}^{a} \\\\\n&=-2 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)[\\frac{1}{t^{4}}]_{2}^{a} \\\\\n&=-2 \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)(\\frac{1}{a^{4}} - \\frac{1}{2^{4}}) \\\\\n&=-2(0 - \\frac{1}{16}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186083007 1074 "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{t}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-4}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-2(0-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186083008 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186083008 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186083008 1074 "Notice that, this time, it's the *lower* limit of integration that's unbounded.\n\nFirst, we substitute $u=x-4$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty$, the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x =\\int_{-\\infty}^{-4} \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{- ∞}^{0}(1)/((x - 4)^{2})dx &= \\int_{- ∞}^{-4}\\frac{1}{u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\int_{- ∞}^{-4}u^{-2}du \\\\\n&= lim_(a → - ∞)\\int_{a}^{-4}u^{-2}du \\\\\n&= lim_(a → - ∞)[- u^{-1}]_{a}^{-4} \\\\\n&=-1 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)[\\frac{1}{u}]_{a}^{-4} \\\\\n&=-1 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)[(1)/((-4)) - \\frac{1}{a}] \\\\\n&=-1 \\cdot lim_(a → - ∞)(-\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{a}) \\\\\n&=-1 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186083008 1074 "Notice that, this time, it's the *lower* limit of integration that's unbounded.  \n\nFirst, we substitute $u=x-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\\int_{-\\infty}^{-4} \\dfrac{1}{u^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186083009 1071 "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x} }\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{-2} \\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186083009 1071 "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{-2} \\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186083009 1074 "Let's work out each integral.\n\n- For the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[2{x}^{1/2}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[\\sqrt{x}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{b}-1] \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\n- For the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{1+x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[-\\frac{1}{1-2}+\\frac{1}{1+a}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{1+a}+1] \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\n- For the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\nIn conclusion, only integral I is divergent." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186083009 1074 "Let's work out each integral.\n\n- For the first integral, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{∞}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}dx|= lim_(b → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}dx; = lim_(b → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{b}x^{-1/2}dx; = lim_(b → ∞)[2x^{1/2}]_{1}^{b}; = 2lim_(b → ∞)[\\sqrt{x}]_{1}^{b}; = 2lim_(b → ∞)[\\sqrt{b} - 1]; = ∞$,\nso the integral diverges.\n- For the second integral, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{-2}(1)/((1 + x)^{2})dx|= lim_(a → - ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{-2}(1)/((1 + x)^{2})dx; = lim_(a → - ∞)[- \\frac{1}{1 + x}]_{a}^{-2}; = lim_(a → - ∞)[- \\frac{1}{1 - 2} + \\frac{1}{1 + a}]; = lim_(a → - ∞)[\\frac{1}{1 + a} + 1]; = 1$,\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n- For the third integral, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{∞}\\frac{1}{x^{3}}dx|= lim_(b → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{x^{3}}dx; = lim_(b → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{b}x^{-3}dx; = lim_(b → ∞)[- \\frac{1}{2x^{2}}]_{1}^{b}; = lim_(b → ∞)[- \\frac{1}{2b^{2}} + (1)/(2(1^{2}))]; = lim_(b → ∞)[\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{2b^{2}}]; = \\frac{1}{2} - 0; = \\frac{1}{2}$,\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\nIn conclusion, only integral I is divergent." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186083012 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186083012 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186083012 1074 "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u = \\cos x$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\textrm d u = \\sin x\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{π/2}\\tan xdx &= \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\frac{\\sin xdx}{\\cos x} \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{0}\\frac{-du}{u} \\\\\n&=-\\int_{1}^{0}\\frac{du}{u} \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{du}{u}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u) = \\dfrac{1}{u} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad u\\rightarrow 0^+\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to0^+$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{du}{u} &= lim_(a → 0^{+})\\int_{a}^{1}\\frac{du}{u} \\\\\n&= \\left.lim_(a → 0^{+})\\ln \\mid u \\mid \\right|_{a}^{1} \\\\\n&= lim_(a → 0^{+})[\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\ln & a\\end{vmatrix}] \\\\\n&= lim_(a → 0^{+})[0 - \\ln \\mid a \\mid ] \\\\\n&= - (- ∞) \\\\\n&= ∞\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186083012 1074 "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u = \\cos x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\textrm d u = \\sin x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{-\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u) = \\dfrac{1}{u} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{ as }\\quad u\\rightarrow 0^+.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to0^+.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & =-(-\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186083013 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola\n$4 y^2 - 10 x^2 - 8 y - 40 x = 76$?" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186083013 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola \n$4 y^2  - 10 x^2 - 8 y  - 40 x  = 76?$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186083013 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4y^{2} - 10x^{2} - 8y - 40x &= 76 \\\\\n4y^{2} - 8y - 10x^{2} - 40x &= 76 \\\\\n4[y^{2} - 2y] - 10[x^{2} + 4x] &= 76 \\\\\n4[(y - 1)^{2} - 1] - 10[(x + 2)^{2} - 4] &= 76 \\\\\n4(y - 1)^{2} - 4 - 10(x + 2)^{2} + 40 &= 76 \\\\\n4(y - 1)^{2} - 10(x + 2)^{2} &= 76 - 40 + 4 \\\\\n4(y - 1)^{2} - 10(x + 2)^{2} &= 40 \\\\\n((y - 1)^{2})/(10) - ((x + 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(-2,1)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186083013 1074 "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}-10{x}^{2}-8y-40x & =76 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-8y-10{x}^{2}-40x & =76 \\\\ 4[{y}^{2}-2y]-10[{x}^{2}+4x] & =76 \\\\ 4[(y-1)^{2}-1]-10[(x+2)^{2}-4] & =76 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-4-10(x+2)^{2}+40 & =76 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-10(x+2)^{2} & =76-40+4 \\\\ 4(y-1)^{2}-10(x+2)^{2} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center is $(-2,1)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186083014 1071 "Compute the arc length of  the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/4e/4ed8b94a4fff3c22697264e467f955f2e51a62618d03295c4e4f38493bff2ced.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186083014 1071 "Compute the arc length of the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/4e/4ed8b94a4fff3c22697264e467f955f2e51a62618d03295c4e4f38493bff2ced.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186083014 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^{3/2}\n$$\n\n$a=0$, and\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt {1 + [f'(x)]^2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{1 + [f^{′}(x)]^{2}} &= \\sqrt{1 + [\\frac{3}{2}x^{1/2}]^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{4}x} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{4 + 9x}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(4 + 9x)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}\\sqrt{4 + 9x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sqrt {1 + [f'(x)]^2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}\n$$\n\nto the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1 + [f^{′}(x)]^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution\n\n$$\nu= 4+9x\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{9}\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ u & 4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{16}\\sqrt{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{9}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}\\int_{4}^{16}u^{\\frac{1}{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}[\\frac{2}{3}u^{\\frac{3}{2}}]_{4}^{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}[u^{\\frac{3}{2}}]_{4}^{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{27}([(16)^{\\frac{3}{2}}] - [(4)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{27}([64] - [8]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{56}{27}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186083014 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)= x^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) =   \\dfrac{3}{2}x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 }\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned