z).$ Since $x = \\dfrac{6}{5}$ and $y = \\dfrac{3}{5}$ are both positive, $z$ lies in the first quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{6}{5})}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =0.464,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. \n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\left (\\cos(0.464)+ \\textrm{i} \\sin(0.464) \\right)\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087528 1071 "Plot the graph of the parametrically defined curve\n\n$$\nx=t(t-6), \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2\\leq t \\leq 2.\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087528 1071 "Plot the graph of the parametrically defined curve\n$x=t(t-6), \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2\\leq t \\leq 2$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087528 1074 "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-6)$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t^3$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/f1/f15f25af0c4804dc811a0ab231ba2ef3ecce2ecd12501a1f2d8b9e34b71a505b.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087528 1074 "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-6).$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t^3.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/f1/f15f25af0c4804dc811a0ab231ba2ef3ecce2ecd12501a1f2d8b9e34b71a505b.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087529 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^2-6t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^4+4, \\quad t \\in (-\\infty , \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087529 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n$x=t^2-6t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^4+4, \\quad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$,\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087529 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x$, we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n$x|= t^{2} - 6t; = (t - 3)^{2} - (3)^{2}; = (t - 3)^{2} - 9$\nSince $\\left(t-3\\right)^2 \\geq 0$, we can conclude that $x \\geq -9$.\n- To find the range of $y=t^4+4$, notice that $t^4 \\geq 0$, and consequently $y \\geq 4$.\n\nTherefore, the ranges are\n\n$$\nx \\geq -9\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\geq 4\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087529 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}-6t \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-(3)^{2} \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left( t-3\\right)^2 \\geq 0,$ we can conclude that $x \\geq -9.$\n\n- To find the range of $y=t^4+4,$ notice that $t^4 \\geq 0,$ and consequently $y \\geq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x \\geq -9$ and $y \\geq 4.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087530 1074 "The Cartesian equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $144$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x^{2} - 16y^{2} &= 144 \\\\\n\\frac{9x^{2}}{144} - \\frac{16y^{2}}{144} &= \\frac{144}{144} \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{16} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087530 1074 "The Cartesian equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to write the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to divide both sides of the equation by $144$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-16{y}^{2} & =144 \\\\ \\frac{9{x}^{2}}{144}-\\frac{16{y}^{2}}{144} & =\\frac{144}{144} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087531 1071 "![](images/45/4527514f6b522bf5922bcba4b77a1574b29f65c238c8b3dd976d6af6bb6c01ae.png)\n\nCalculate the points where the ellipse $x^2 + 3y^2 - 4x -  6y = 2$ (shown above) intercepts the $x$-axis." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087531 1071 "![](images/45/4527514f6b522bf5922bcba4b77a1574b29f65c238c8b3dd976d6af6bb6c01ae.png)\n\nCalculate the points where the ellipse $x^2 + 3y^2 - 4x - 6y = 2$ (shown above) intercepts the $x$-axis." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087531 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x$. We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 3y^{2} - 4x - 6y &= 2 \\\\\nx^{2} + 3 \\cdot 0^{2} - 4x - 6 \\cdot 0 &= 2 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x &= 2 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 4x + 4) - 4 &= 2 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} - 4 &= 2 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} &= 6 \\\\\nx - 2 &= ± \\sqrt{6} \\\\\nx &= 2 ± \\sqrt{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{6},0)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087531 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4x-6y & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}-4x-6\\cdot 0 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x+4)-4 & =2 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =6 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(2-\\sqrt{6},0)$ and $(2+\\sqrt{6},0).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087532 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 2)^{2} + ((y - 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} + ((0 - 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} + 1 &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx - 2 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(2,0)$.\n\n![](images/db/db1ffc11b3321e87f7c5d2cb490d0381f4b950dfcfd64be00f49c07577f797c0.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087532 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+1 & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](images/db/db1ffc11b3321e87f7c5d2cb490d0381f4b950dfcfd64be00f49c07577f797c0.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087533 1071 "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $25x^2 + 9y^2 - 100x - 18y = 116$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087533 1071 "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $25x^2 + 9y^2 - 100x - 18y = 116.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087533 1074 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25x^{2} + 9y^{2} - 100x - 18y &= 116 \\\\\n(25x^{2} - 100x) + (9y^{2} - 18y) &= 116\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(25x^{2} - 100x) + (9y^{2} - 18y) &= 116 \\\\\n25[x^{2} - 4x] + 9[y^{2} - 2y] &= 116 \\\\\n25[(x - 2)^{2} - 4] + 9[(y - 1)^{2} - 1] &= 116 \\\\\n25(x - 2)^{2} - 100 + 9(y - 1)^{2} - 9 &= 116\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25(x - 2)^{2} - 100 + 9(y - 1)^{2} - 9 &= 116 \\\\\n25(x - 2)^{2} + 9(y - 1)^{2} &= 116 + 100 + 9 \\\\\n25(x - 2)^{2} + 9(y - 1)^{2} &= 225 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(9) + ((y - 1)^{2})/(25) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(3^{2}) + ((y - 1)^{2})/(5^{2}) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5 > 3$, the length of the major axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 5 =10\n$$\n\nthe length of the minor axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3 =6\n$$\n\nand the ellipse is centered at $({2},{1})$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087533 1074 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-100x-18y & =116 \\\\ (25{x}^{2}-100x)+(9{y}^{2}-18y) & =116\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25{x}^{2}-100x)+(9{y}^{2}-18y) & =116 \\\\ 25[{x}^{2}-4x]+9[{y}^{2}-2y] & =116 \\\\ 25[(x-2)^{2}-4]+9[(y-1)^{2}-1] & =116 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}-100+9(y-1)^{2}-9 & =116\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25(x-2)^{2}-100+9(y-1)^{2}-9 & =116 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}+9(y-1)^{2} & =116+100+9 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}+9(y-1)^{2} & =225 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5 > 3,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 5 =10,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 3 =6,$ and the ellipse is centered at $({2},{1}).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087534 1071 "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $x^2 + 4y^2 - 6x + 24y + 41 = 0$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087534 1071 "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $x^2 + 4y^2 - 6x + 24y + 41 = 0.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087534 1074 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 4y^{2} - 6x + 24y + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 6x) + (4y^{2} + 24y) + 41 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2} - 6x) + (4y^{2} + 24y) + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 6x) + 4[y^{2} + 6y] + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 4[(y + 3)^{2} - 9] + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 4(y + 3)^{2} - 36 + 41 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 4(y + 3)^{2} - 36 + 41 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} + 4(y + 3)^{2} &= 9 + 36 - 41 \\\\\n(x - 3)^{2} + 4(y + 3)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n((x - 3)^{2})/(4) + ((y + 3)^{2})/(1) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 3)^{2})/(2^{2}) + ((y + 3)^{2})/(1^{2}) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2 > 1$, the length of the major axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 2 =4\n$$\n\nthe length of the minor axis is\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 1 =2\n$$\n\nand the ellipse is centered at $({3},{-3})$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087534 1074 "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-6x+24y+41 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x)+(4{y}^{2}+24y)+41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-6x)+(4{y}^{2}+24y)+41 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x)+4[{y}^{2}+6y]+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+4[(y+3)^{2}-9]+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+4(y+3)^{2}-36+41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}-9+4(y+3)^{2}-36+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4(y+3)^{2} & =9+36-41 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4(y+3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2 > 1,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2 =4,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 1 =2,$ and the ellipse is centered at $({3},{-3}).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087535 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(1,-1)$, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$, and the vertical radius has length $b=3$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - h)^{2})/(a^{2}) + ((y - k)^{2})/(b^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 1)^{2})/(5^{2}) + ((y - (-1))^{2})/(3^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 1)^{2})/(25) + ((y + 1)^{2})/(9) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087535 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(1,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=5,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087536 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - 1)^{2})/(4) + ((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1 \\\\\n((0 - 1)^{2})/(4) + ((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4} + ((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1 \\\\\n((y - 3)^{2})/(16) &= \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\n(y - 3)^{2} &= 12 \\\\\ny - 3 &= ± \\sqrt{12} \\\\\ny - 3 &= 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\\ny &= 3 ± 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,3 -2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,3 +2\\sqrt{3})$.\n\n![](images/c0/c090d0410cca8d00f0f93641a4f8bfbfa66c3b586894797e5c5c89291d11126d.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087536 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =12 \\\\ y-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{12} \\\\ y-3 & =2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =3\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $(0,3 -2\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,3 +2\\sqrt{3}).$\n\n![](images/c0/c090d0410cca8d00f0f93641a4f8bfbfa66c3b586894797e5c5c89291d11126d.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087537 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y$. We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 20x - 8y + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 0^{2} + 2y^{2} - 20 \\cdot 0 - 8y + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2y^{2} - 8y + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2(y^{2} - 4y) + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2[y^{2} - 4y + 4 - 4] + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2[(y - 2)^{2} - 4] + 13 &= 0 \\\\\n2(y - 2)^{2} + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= -\\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n(y-2)^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\n, there is no $y$ such that\n\n$$\n(y-2)^2 = -\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point.\n\n![](images/dc/dc56aec7e7c16c4fcfee77f7126f53351c1513c4bfb5e64ac41834ed30ee5782.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087537 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x = 0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20x-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 5\\cdot {0}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20\\cdot 0-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-8y+13 & =0 \\\\ 2({y}^{2}-4y)+13 & =0 \\\\ 2[{y}^{2}-4y+4-4]+13 & =0 \\\\ 2[(y-2)^{2}-4]+13 & =0 \\\\ 2{(y-2)}^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-2)^2 \\geq 0$, there is no $y$ such that $(y-2)^2 = -\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point.\n\n![](images/dc/dc56aec7e7c16c4fcfee77f7126f53351c1513c4bfb5e64ac41834ed30ee5782.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087538 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,-3)$, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$, and the vertical radius has length $b=4$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - h)^{2})/(a^{2}) + ((y - k)^{2})/(b^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - (-1))^{2})/(2^{2}) + ((y - (-3))^{2})/(4^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x + 1)^{2})/(4) + ((y + 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087538 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,-3),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-3))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087539 1071 "For the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{25} + \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{16} = 1$, find its center, and the lengths of its major and minor axes." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087539 1071 "For the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{25} + \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{16} = 1,$ find its center, and the lengths of its major and minor axes." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087539 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=2$ and $k=-2$, so the center is located at $(2,-2)$.\n\nIn the given equation, we also have $a^2=25$ and $b^2=16$.\n\n- Since $a^2=25$, the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{25} = 5$.\n- Since $b^2=16$, the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16} = 4$.\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=5$, and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4$.\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length\n\n$$\n2a = 2\\cdot5 = 10\n$$\n\nand the minor axis has length\n\n$$\n2b = 2\\cdot4 = 8\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087539 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=2$ and $k=-2,$ so the center is located at $(2,-2).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have $a^2=25$ and $b^2=16.$\n\n- Since $a^2=25,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{25} = 5.$\n\n- Since $b^2=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16} = 4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a = 2\\cdot5 = 10,$ and the minor axis has length $2b = 2\\cdot4 = 8.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087540 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z=3+6\\textrm{i}$?" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087540 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z=3+6\\textrm{i}?$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087540 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number\n\n$$\nz=3+6\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe imaginary part is $6\\textrm{i}$. Flipping the sign, we get $-6\\textrm{i}$.\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{z} = 3-6\\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087540 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=3+6\\textrm{i},$ the imaginary part is $6\\textrm{i}.$ Flipping the sign, we get $-6\\textrm{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overline{z} = 3-6\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087541 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z=-4-6\\textrm{i}$?" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087541 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z=-4-6\\textrm{i}?$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087541 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number\n\n$$\nz=-4-6\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe imaginary part is $-6\\textrm{i}$. Flipping the sign, we get $6\\textrm{i}$.\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{z} = -4+6\\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087541 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=-4-6\\textrm{i},$ the imaginary part is $-6\\textrm{i}.$ Flipping the sign, we get $6\\textrm{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overline{z} = -4+6\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087542 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z=7\\textrm{i}$?" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087542 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z=7\\textrm{i}?$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087542 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number\n\n$$\nz=7\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe imaginary part is $7\\textrm{i}$ (there is no real part, just an imaginary part). Flipping the sign, we get $-7\\textrm{i}$.\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{z} = -7\\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087542 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=7\\textrm{i},$ the imaginary part is $7\\textrm{i}$ (there is no real part, just an imaginary part). Flipping the sign, we get $-7\\textrm{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overline{z} = -7\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087543 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z=-6$?" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087543 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z=-6?$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087543 1074 "The number $z=-6$ is a real number, and the complex conjugate of any real number is just the number itself.\n\nSo here, the complex conjugate is\n\n$$\n\\overline{z} = z = -6\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087543 1074 "The number $z=-6$ is a real number, and the complex conjugate of any real number is just the number itself.\n\nSo here, the complex conjugate is $\\overline{z} = z =  -6.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087544 1071 "If $z=7-3\\textrm{i}$, calculate $z+\\overline{z}$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087544 1071 "If $z=7-3\\textrm{i}$, calculate $z+\\overline{z}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087544 1074 "*Method 1*\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number\n\n$$\nz=7-3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe real part is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Re}(z) = 7\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz + z^(―) &= 2Re(z) \\\\\n&= 2(7) \\\\\n&= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Method 2*\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number\n\n$$\nz=7-3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nwe flip the sign of the imaginary part and get\n\n$$\n\\overline{z} = 7 + 3 \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz + z^(―) &= (7 - 3\\textrm{i}) + (7 + 3\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= (7 + 7) + (-3\\textrm{i} + 3\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= 14 + \\textrm{i}0 \\\\\n&= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087544 1074 "*Method 1*\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=7-3\\textrm{i},$ the real part is $\\operatorname{Re}(z) = 7,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(7) \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Method 2*\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=7-3\\textrm{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overline{z} = 7 + 3 \\textrm{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(7-3\\text{i})+(7+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(7+7)+(-3\\text{i}+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =14+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087545 1071 "Given that $z=7-4\\textrm{i}$, calculate $z\\overline{z}$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087545 1071 "Given that $z=7-4\\textrm{i},$ calculate $z\\overline{z}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087545 1074 "*Method 1*\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number\n\n$$\nz=7-4\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe real part is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Re}(z)=7\n$$\n\nand the imaginary part is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Im}(z)=-4\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nzz^(―) &= (Re(z))^{2} + (Im(z))^{2} \\\\\n&= 7^{2} + (-4)^{2} \\\\\n&= 49 + 16 \\\\\n&= 65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Method 2*\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number\n\n$$\nz=7-4\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nwe flip the sign of the imaginary part and get\n\n$$\n\\overline{z} = 7 + 4 \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nzz^(―) &= (7 - 4\\textrm{i})(7 + 4\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&= 7^{2} + (7)(4)\\textrm{i} + (-4)(7)\\textrm{i} - (4)^{2}\\textrm{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= 49 + 28\\textrm{i} - 28\\textrm{i} - (16)(-1) \\\\\n&= 49 + 16 \\\\\n&= 65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087545 1074 "*Method 1*\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=7-4\\textrm{i},$ the real part is $\\textrm{Re}(z)=7,$ and the imaginary part is $\\textrm{Im}(z)=-4,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(\\text{Re}(z))^{2}+(\\text{Im}(z))^{2} \\\\  & ={7}^{2}+(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =49+16 \\\\  & =65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Method 2*\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=7-4\\textrm{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overline{z} = 7 + 4 \\textrm{i}.$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(7-4\\text{i})(7+4\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={7}^{2}+(7)(4)\\text{i}+(-4)(7)\\text{i}-(4)^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =49+28\\text{i}-28\\text{i}-(16)(-1) \\\\  & =49+16 \\\\  & =65.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087546 1071 "Write the complex number ![](images/c9/c93c5bf19eefeb4da980b81d46c4df10b3ff10aca21937aed885ef4111b1c9a7.png), shown in the following Argand diagram, in exponential form.\n\n![](images/33/335311049419928acc946721242c6e8e4096313dca1290b89b1cc7a2c30748fd.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087546 1071 "Write the complex number $z$, shown in the following Argand diagram, in exponential form.\n\n![](images/33/335311049419928acc946721242c6e8e4096313dca1290b89b1cc7a2c30748fd.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087546 1074 "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n![](images/5d/5d6456ae3c5f168a14e761b44085defd76cd2292e8fa8d9b0eedf2cb55d97563.png)\n\nTo write it in the form ![](images/0e/0eb9bfc5066fce3fe0434f4dedf05cea2fff1e2fe38b32d4223eaf177ca2b654.png) where ![](images/d3/d3e8106a9b65dceed9a9c4e6fbc6341c0268517854b72f6e34fc3ec80117b915.png) and ![](images/60/6017ce5c562b2c1a2d1a47371c54820b04852fb851056aa171f88c411d92bd45.png) we first calculate ![](images/e3/e3862c77b505ea48e95acf4d632fa692c866c65079a2ac6b17543fd54e1c20ca.png)\n![](images/d0/d0bd5d7fc7f1d7b487fd940af95b5abb7e2fb134c02a55dbf9eb7d191abccca8.png)\n\nNow, since ![](images/c9/c93c5bf19eefeb4da980b81d46c4df10b3ff10aca21937aed885ef4111b1c9a7.png) lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle ![](images/f4/f49e0503f6588c93dd1fd274c63f60c06b0eb77d5dce2d12dba750107139f124.png) as\n![](images/96/96e40550e19a81ffb655cd359242d8b0a37b1dde12e442252f976397a09a9760.png)\n\nto three decimal places.\n\nFinally, substituting ![](images/5d/5dcf5f8d9a0eb6d36f76152105bcb5a9af61a34bc99719183df2044d7f113edb.png) and ![](images/11/11dd78699cf02b8b10f1bd6dc78ee987d26954fc8b51a2e8327c2f24b5552458.png) into ![](images/9e/9ea3d5e030449e3b1da133b0aa004c6d26702021bf66e5672abe2bdd2c69eb9b.png) we get \n![](images/dd/dd0d831b250bbf985783cbb745a23c709a481ba08018395c683c3e2d4b00284a.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087546 1074 "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= x + \\textrm{i}y \\\\\n&=-3 + 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form\n\n$$\nz = r\\textrm{e}^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nr= \\mid z \\mid\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arg(z)\n$$\n\nwe first calculate $r$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr = \\mid z \\mid &= \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-3)^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\arg (z) &= π - \\mid \\arctan (\\frac{y}{x}) \\mid \\\\\n&= π - \\mid \\arctan (\\frac{2}{3}) \\mid \\\\\n&= π - 0.588 \\\\\n&= 2.554\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places.\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{13}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta=2.554\n$$\n\ninto\n\n$$\nz=re^{\\textrm{i} \\theta}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nz = \\sqrt{13}\\,\\textrm{e}^{2.554\\,\\textrm{i}}\\,\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087547 1074 "To write our complex number in the form\n\n$$\nz = r\\textrm{e}^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nr= \\mid z \\mid\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arg(z)\n$$\n\nwe first calculate $r$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr = \\mid z \\mid &= \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ = \\arg (z) &= \\arctan (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\arctan (1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nr=4\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ninto\n\n$$\nz=re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nz = 4\\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{e}^{\\pi\\textrm{i}/4}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087547 1074 "To write our complex number in the form $z = r\\textrm{e}^{\\textrm{i}\\theta},$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta = \\arg(z),$ we first calculate $r\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the first quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into $z=re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta},$ we get\n\n$$\nz = 4\\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{e}^{\\pi\\textrm{i}/4}.\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087548 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos\\theta, \\,\\,\\, y = 2\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <     2\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087548 1071 "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n$x = 4\\cos\\theta, \\,\\,\\, y = 2\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi$,\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087548 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x$, notice that for $0\\leq\\theta < 2\\pi$, we have $-1 \\leq \\cos{\\theta} \\leq 1$. Consequently,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\le \\cos θ \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\cos θ \\le 4 \\\\ -4 & \\le x \\le 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSo, we can conclude that $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4$.\n- To find the range of $y$, notice that for $0\\leq\\theta < 2\\pi$, we have $-1 \\leq \\sin{\\theta} \\leq 1$. Consequently,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\le \\sin θ \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\sin θ \\le 2 \\\\ -2 & \\le y \\le 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSo, we can conclude that $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2$.\n\nTherefore, the ranges are\n\n$$\n-4 \\leq x \\leq 4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq y \\leq 2\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087548 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\leq\\theta <  2\\pi,$ we have $-1 \\leq \\cos{\\theta} \\leq 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 4 \\\\ -4 & \\le x\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\leq\\theta <  2\\pi,$ we have $-1 \\leq \\sin{\\theta} \\leq 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2 & \\le y\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4$ and $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087549 1071 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-x-6}$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087549 1071 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-x-6}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087549 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x .$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)(x-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x = -2$ and $x = 3.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x = -2$ and $x = 3.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087549 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1)/((x + 2)(x - 3))\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 2)(x - 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x = -2$ and $x = 3$. Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x = -2$ and $x = 3$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087550 1071 "Find the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{2}{x^3+x^2+4x}$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087550 1071 "Find the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{2}{x^3+x^2+4x}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087550 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2}{x^{3} + x^{2} + 4x} \\\\\n&= (2)/(x(x^{2} + x + 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(x^2 +x+ 4) = 0\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x = 0$ gives the solution $x = 0$.\n- The equation $x^2 +x+ 4 = 0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n$D|= b^{2} - 4ac; = (1)^{2} - 4(1)(4); = 1 - 16; =-15$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x = 0$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087550 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x({x}^{2}+x+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(x^2 +x+ 4) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x = 0$ gives the solution $x = 0.$\n\n- The equation $x^2 +x+ 4 = 0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(1{)}^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =1-16 \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x = 0.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087551 1071 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x-2}{x^2-6x+8}$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087551 1071 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x-2}{x^2-6x+8}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087551 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x - 2}{x^{2} - 6x + 8} \\\\\n&= (x - 2)/((x - 2)(x - 4)) \\\\\n&= (x - 2)/((x - 2)(x - 4)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x - 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n$x - 4 = 0$\n\nThis gives the solution $x = 4$. Therefore, $x = 4$ is the only vertical asymptote." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087551 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-6x+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x = 4.$ Therefore, $x = 4$ is the only vertical asymptote." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087552 1071 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-x-6}{x^3 - 3 x^2 + x - 3}$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087552 1071 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-x-6}{x^3 - 3 x^2 + x - 3}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186087552 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{2} - x - 6}{x^{3} - 3x^{2} + x - 3} \\\\\n&= ((x + 2)(x - 3))/(x^{2}(x - 3) + (x - 3)) \\\\\n&= ((x + 2)(x - 3))/((x^{2} + 1)(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= ((x + 2)(x - 3))/((x^{2} + 1)(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x + 2}{x^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= b^{2} - 4ac \\\\\n&= (0)^{2} - 4(1)(1) \\\\\n&= 0 - 4 \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186087552 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{a