rac{4}{9}$, find $P(B)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186096521 1071 "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $P(A\\cup B) = \\dfrac{4}{9},$ find $P(B).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186096521 1074 "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∪B) &= P(A) + P(B) \\\\\nP(B) &= P(A∪B) - P(A) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{9} - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 3}{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186096521 1074 "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B) = 0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(B) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-3}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186096522 1071 "Two cards are drawn from a standard ![](images/c1/c1a5898916d3156aa9acb1884f2ab9fd149c10ca2149574a50fc6147586691e6.png)-card deck: the ace of hearts and the queen of hearts. These are set aside, and a third card is drawn from the remaining deck. What is the probability that the third drawn card is an ace or a heart?\n\n*Hint: A standard ![](images/5f/5f378bdc2525ccdfd98ae36dc698e3bdf3f328a76d6841faf2198ec37b34db9a.png)-card deck has ![](images/d4/d44ac804f533a60a0d252fbcddaec9693d8b989dc429bf03f3ef1f0587cf3453.png) suits with ![](images/f3/f3ae95c18ff411808c146e5d3bfc30f49380b8272511f1a90a3400209b2df340.png) cards in each suit.There is one ace in each suit.\n Hearts is one of the suits.*" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186096522 1071 "Two cards are drawn from a standard $52$-card deck: the ace of hearts and the queen of hearts. These are set apart, and a third card is drawn from the remaining deck. What is the probability that the third drawn card is an ace or a heart?" 13