H)$.\n\nGiven that the ace of hearts is no longer in the deck, $A$ and $H$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e.,\n\n$$\nP(A \\cap H) = 0\n$$\n\nWe can find $P(A)$ and $P(H)$ by computing the necessary ratios. When the third card is drawn, there are $50$ cards in the deck. Among the remaining $50$ cards, there are $13-2=11$ hearts and $4-1=3$ aces. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= \\frac{3}{50} \\\\\nP(H) &= \\frac{11}{50}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∪H) &= P(A) + P(H) - P(A∩H) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{50} + \\frac{11}{50} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{14}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186096530 1071 "Given $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3}$, $P(B)=\\dfrac{1}{4}$, and $P(A\\cup B)=\\dfrac{7}{12}$, determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186096530 1071 "Given $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3},$ $P(B)=\\dfrac{1}{4},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\dfrac{7}{12},$ determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186096530 1074 "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B) = 0\n$$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∪B) &= P(A) + P(B) - P(A∩B) \\\\\n\\frac{7}{12} &= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{4} - P(A∩B) \\\\\nP(A∩B) &= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{7}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 + 3 - 7}{12} \\\\\n&= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186096530 1074 "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B) = 0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{7}{12} & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{7}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+3-7}{12} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100719 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100719 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n$x=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100719 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t$. First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2$:\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting\n\n$$\nt^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\ninto our expression for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= t^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nt \\geq0\n$$\n\nand $x=2t^2$, we must have\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100719 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100720 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n$x=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100720 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100720 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100720 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x = \\csc t & ⇒\\csc t = x \\\\ y = 2\\cot t & ⇒\\cot t = \\frac{y}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1\n$$\n\nby $\\sin^{2}{t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sin^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t} + \\frac{\\cos^{2} t}{\\sin^{2} t} &= \\frac{1}{\\sin^{2} t} \\\\\n1 + (\\frac{\\cos t}{\\sin t})^{2} &= (\\frac{1}{\\sin t})^{2} \\\\\n1 + \\cot^{2} t &= \\csc^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and\n\n$$\n\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}\n$$\n\ninto this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + (\\frac{y}{2})^{2} &= x^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} &= x^{2} - 1 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4(x^{2} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is\n\n$$\ny^2 = 4\\left(x^2-1 \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand $x=\\csc t$, the interval on the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100723 1071 "Expand ${( 3x + y )}^3$ using the binomial theorem." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100723 1071 "Expand ${(3x + y)}^3$ using the binomial theorem." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100723 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n$, we have\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\cdots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(3x +y)}^3$, we have $a=3x$, $b=y$, and $n=3$.\n\nTherefore, we can expand ${(3x + y)}^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3x + y)^{3} &= (\\frac{3}{0})(3x)^{3}(y)^{0} + (\\frac{3}{1})(3x)^{3 - 1}(y)^{1} + (\\frac{3}{2})(3x)^{3 - 2}(y)^{2} + (\\frac{3}{3})(3x)^{3 - 3}(y)^{3} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{3}{0})(3x)^{3}(1) + (\\frac{3}{1})(3x)^{2}(y)^{1} + (\\frac{3}{2})(3x)^{1}(y)^{2} + (\\frac{3}{3})(3x)^{0}(y)^{3} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot (3x)^{3} + 3 \\cdot (3x)^{2}(y) + 3 \\cdot (3x)(y)^{2} + 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (y^{3}) \\\\\n&= 27x^{3} + 27x^{2}y + 9xy^{2} + y^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100723 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(3x +y)}^3,$ we have  $a=3x,$ $b=y,$ and $n=3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can expand ${(3x + y)}^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x+y)^{3} & =(\\frac{3}{0})(3x)^{3}(y)^{0}+(\\frac{3}{1})(3x)^{3-1}(y)^{1}+(\\frac{3}{2})(3x)^{3-2}(y)^{2}+(\\frac{3}{3})(3x)^{3-3}(y)^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{0})(3x)^{3}(1)+(\\frac{3}{1})(3x)^{2}(y)^{1}+(\\frac{3}{2})(3x)^{1}(y)^{2}+(\\frac{3}{3})(3x)^{0}(y)^{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (3x)^{3}+3\\cdot (3x)^{2}(y)+3\\cdot (3x)(y)^{2}+1\\cdot 1\\cdot ({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =27{x}^{3}+27{x}^{2}y+9x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100724 1071 "Find the coefficient of $x^{4}$ in the expansion of ${\\left( {3 + x} \\right)^{7}} .$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100724 1071 "Find the coefficient of $x^{4}$ in the expansion of ${\\left({3 + x} \\right)^{7}}$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100724 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n$, we have\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\cdots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}\n$$\n\nIn the case of\n\n$$\n{\\left({3 +x} \\right)^{7}}\n$$\n\nwe have $a=3$, $b=x$, and $n=7$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n(3 + x)^{7} = (\\frac{7}{0})3^{7}x^{0} + (\\frac{7}{1})3^{7 - 1}x^{1} + (\\frac{7}{2})3^{7 - 2}x^{2} + ⋯ + (\\frac{7}{7})3^{7 - 7}x^{7}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the $x^4$ term must be\n\n$$\n{7 \\choose 4}{3^{7 - 4}}{x^4}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{7}{4})3^{7 - 4} &= 35 \\cdot 3^{3} \\\\\n&= 35 \\cdot 27 \\\\\n&= 945\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of $x^{4}$ is $945$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100724 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${\\left( {3 +x} \\right)^{7}},$ we have $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+x)^{7}=(\\frac{7}{0}){3}^{7}{x}^{0}+(\\frac{7}{1}){3}^{7-1}{x}^{1}+(\\frac{7}{2}){3}^{7-2}{x}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{7}{7}){3}^{7-7}{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the $x^4$ term must be\n\n$$\n{7 \\choose 4}{3^{7 - 4}}{x^4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{7}{4}){3}^{7-4} & =35\\cdot {3}^{3} \\\\  & =35\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =945.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of $x^{4}$ is $945.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100731 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100731 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n$y' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0$\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n2. Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n3. The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100731 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$, we get\n$f^{′}(x)|= (\\frac{1}{x})^{′}; =-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}$.\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n$y^{′}|=^(?) - \\frac{y}{x}; - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}|=^(?) - ((\\frac{1}{x}))/(x); - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}|=-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}✓$\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100731 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100732 1071 "Find the values of $\\theta$ at which the curve $x=\\cos \\theta, \\, y = 2 \\sin \\theta, \\, 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi$ intersects the line $2x-y=0$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100732 1071 "Find the values of $\\theta$ at which the curve $x=\\cos \\theta, \\, y = 2 \\sin \\theta, \\, 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi$ intersects the line $2x-y=0.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100732 1074 "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= 0 \\\\\n2\\cos θ - 2\\sin θ &= 0 \\\\\n2\\cos θ &= 2\\sin θ \\\\\n\\cos θ &= \\sin θ \\\\\n1 &= \\frac{\\sin θ}{\\cos θ} \\\\\n1 &= \\tan θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan(1) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nso our first solution is\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi$ to $\\theta_1$:\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} + \\pi = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\,\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100732 1074 "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan(1) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\leq \\theta <     2\\pi,$ so our first solution is $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi$ to $\\theta_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} + \\pi = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\,\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100733 1071 "Find the points where the curve $x=t^2,y=2t$ intersects the line $4x-2y-15=0$.\n\n![](images/ba/ba682be1ab26bad257fe814020469696f5c302a765a67c45c341762ac9418108.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100733 1071 "Find the points where the curve $x=t^2,y=2t$ intersects the line $4x-2y-15=0.$ \n\n![](images/ba/ba682be1ab26bad257fe814020469696f5c302a765a67c45c341762ac9418108.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100733 1074 "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we are expecting two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t$. Upon finding $t$, we then proceed to find the coordinates of the intersection points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n4(t^{2}) - 2(2t) - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n4t^{2} - 4t - 15 &= 0 \\\\\n(2t - 5)(2t + 3) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= \\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the values of $t$, we proceed to find $x$ and $y$:\n\n- We start with $t=\\dfrac{5}{2}$. For $x$, we have\n$x|= t^{2}; x|= (\\frac{5}{2})^{2}; = \\frac{25}{4}$.\nFor $y$, we have\n$y|= 2t; = 2 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2}; = 5$.\nSo the coordinates of the first intersection point are $\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4},5\\right)$.\n- We now move over to $t=-\\dfrac{3}{2}$. For $x$, we have\n$x|= t^{2}; x|= (-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}; = \\frac{9}{4}$.\nFor $y$, we have\n$y|= 2t; = 2(-\\frac{3}{2}); =-3$.\nSo the coordinates of the second intersection point are $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4},-3\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the curve $x=t^2,y=2t$ intersects the line $4x-2y-15=0$ at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4},5\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4},-3\\right)\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100733 1074 "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we are expecting two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ Upon finding $t,$ we then proceed to find the coordinates of the intersection points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y-15 & =0 \\\\ 4({t}^{2})-2(2t)-15 & =0 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}-4t-15 & =0 \\\\ (2t-5)(2t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the values of $t,$ we proceed to find $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- We start with $t=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2} \\\\ x & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2t \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coordinates of the first intersection point are $\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4},5\\right).$\n\n- We now move over to $t=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2} \\\\ x & ={(-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2t \\\\  & =2(-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coordinates of the second intersection point are $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4},-3\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the curve $x=t^2,y=2t$ intersects the line $4x-2y-15=0$  at the points $\\left(\\dfrac{25}{4},5\\right)$ and $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4},-3\\right).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100734 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y' = 5y$ that satisfies $y(0)=3$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100734 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $y' = 5y$ that satisfies $y(0)=3.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100734 1074 "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 5y \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{dy}{dx} &= 5 \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= 5\\intdx \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{y}dy &= 5\\intdx \\\\\n\\ln \\mid y \\mid &= 5x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= ± e^{5x + C} \\\\\n&= ± e^{C}e^{5x} \\\\\n&= Ke^{5x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm e^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow we apply the condition $y(0) = 3$ to find $K$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(0) &= 3 \\\\\nKe^{5 \\cdot 0} &= 3 \\\\\nK &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $y' = 5y$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=3$ is\n\n$$\ny = 3 e^{5x} \\,\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100734 1074 "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =5\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =5x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm {e}^{5x+C} \\\\  & =\\pm {e}^{C}{e}^{5x} \\\\  & =K{e}^{5x}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm e^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNow we apply the condition $y(0) = 3$ to find $K\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3 \\\\ K{e}^{5\\cdot 0} & =3 \\\\ K & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $y' = 5y$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=3$ is\n\n$$\ny = 3 e^{5x}  \\,.\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100738 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\cos(3y)\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\sin(2x) = 0$ given that  $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0.$ Assume that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} < y < \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} .$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100738 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\cos(3y)\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\sin(2x) = 0$ given that $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0$. Assume that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} < y < \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100738 1074 "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (3y)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\sin (2x) \\\\\n\\int\\cos (3y)\\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= -\\int\\sin (2x)dx \\\\\n\\int\\cos (3y)dy &= -\\int\\sin (2x)dx \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3}\\sin (3y) &= \\frac{1}{2}\\cos (2x) + C \\\\\n\\sin (3y) &= \\frac{3}{2}\\cos (2x) + C_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1=3C$.\n\nNow let's apply the condition\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nto find $C_1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(\\frac{π}{4}) &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin (3 \\cdot 0) &= \\frac{3}{2}\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{4}) + C_{1} \\\\\n\\sin (0) &= \\frac{3}{2}\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) + C_{1} \\\\\n0 &= 0 + C_{1} \\\\\nC_{1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\sin(3y) = \\dfrac {3} {2} \\cos(2x)\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100738 1074 "We first find the general solution, and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1=3C.$\n\nNow let's apply the condition $y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 0$ to find $C_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{1} \\\\ 0 & =0+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\sin(3y)   =  \\dfrac {3} {2} \\cos(2x)\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100739 1071 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for integrals for  $f(x) = x^2-2x+2$ on the interval $[0,3].$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100739 1071 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for integrals for $f(x) = x^2-2x+2$ on the interval $[0,3]$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100739 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(c) (b-a) & = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx \\\\[5pt] f(c) & = \\dfrac {1}{b-a} \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac {1}{3-0} \\int_0^3 (x^2-2x+2) \\, \\textrm dx \\\\[5pt] & = \\left. \\dfrac {1}{3} \\cdot \\left[\\dfrac{x^3}{3} -x^2 +2x\\right] \\right|_0^3 \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac {1}{3} \\cdot \\left[ \\left(\\dfrac{3^3}{3} -3^2 +2\\cdot3\\right) - \\left(\\dfrac{0^3}{3} -0^2 +2\\cdot0\\right)\\right] \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac {1}{3}\\cdot6 \\\\[5pt] & = 2. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(c) &= 2 \\\\\nc^{2} - 2c + 2 &= 2 \\\\\nc(c - 2) &= 0 \\\\\nc &= 0, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100739 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n       f(c) (b-a)  & =  \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx \\[5pt]\n              f(c)    & = \\dfrac {1}{b-a} \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx \\[5pt]\n    & = \\dfrac {1}{3-0} \\int_0^3  (x^2-2x+2) \\, \\textrm dx \\[5pt]\n     & =  \\left . \\dfrac {1}{3} \\cdot  \\left[\\dfrac{x^3}{3} -x^2 +2x\\right] \\right|_0^3 \\[5pt]\n    & =   \\dfrac {1}{3} \\cdot \\left[ \\left(\\dfrac{3^3}{3} -3^2 +2\\cdot3\\right) -  \\left(\\dfrac{0^3}{3} -0^2 +2\\cdot0\\right)\\right] \\[5pt]\n    & =  \\dfrac {1}{3}\\cdot6 \\[5pt]\n    &  = 2.\n}\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =2 \\\\ {c}^{2}-2c+2 & =2 \\\\ c(c-2) & =0 \\\\ c & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100740 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x) = x - 2$ on the interval $[-2, 4]$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100740 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x) = x - 2$ on the interval $[-2, 4].$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100740 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{avg} &= \\frac{1}{b - a}\\int_{a}^{b}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= (1)/(4 - (-2))\\int_{-2}^{4}x - 2dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{x^{2}}{2} - 2x)\\right|_{-2}^{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}[(\\frac{4^{2}}{2} - 2(4)) - (((-2)^{2})/(2) - 2(-2))] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}[(8 - 8) - (2 + 4)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(0 - 6) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100740 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\,{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-(-2)}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}x-2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2x){|}_{-2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-2(4))-(\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{2}-2(-2))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(8-8)-(2+4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(0-6) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100741 1071 "Find $c$ such that the average value of $g(x) = 3 x$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $3$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100741 1071 "Find $c$ such that the average value of $g(x) = 3 x$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $3.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100741 1074 "To solve for $c$, we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_{avg} &= \\frac{1}{b - a}\\int_{a}^{b}g(x)dx \\\\\n3 &= \\frac{1}{c - 0}\\int_{0}^{c}3xdx \\\\\n3 &= \\left.\\frac{1}{c}(\\frac{3x^{2}}{2})\\right|_{0}^{c} \\\\\n3 &= \\frac{1}{c}(\\frac{3c^{2}}{2} - 0) \\\\\n3 &= \\frac{3c}{2} \\\\\nc &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100741 1074 "To solve for $c,$ we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\,{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{c-0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{c}3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{c}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}){|}_{0}^{c} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{c}(\\frac{3{c}^{2}}{2}-0) \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{3c}{2} \\\\ c & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100742 1071 "The graph of the function $f(x)$, which is defined on $[1,6]$, is shown below. The areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5$ and $2$, respectively (from left to right). Find the average value of $f(x)$ on $[1,6]$.\n\n![](images/64/6433d1ca57ceb334f304e894e2655ef50d987567fbcf219314302bb381fd9a83.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100742 1071 "The graph of the function $f(x),$ which is defined on $[1,6],$ is shown below. The areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5$ and $2,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the average value of $f(x)$ on $[1,6].$ \n\n![](images/64/6433d1ca57ceb334f304e894e2655ef50d987567fbcf219314302bb381fd9a83.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186100742 1074 "The average value of a function on a given interval is\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{avg} = \\dfrac{1}{b-a}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nHere, we see that\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{6} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -5 + 2 = -3\n$$\n\nSo, applying the average value formula, we find that the average value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{avg} &= \\frac{1}{b - a}\\int_{a}^{b}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6 - 1} \\cdot (-3) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186100742 1074 "The average value of a function on a given interval is\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{avg} = \\dfrac{1}{b-a}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, we see that\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{6} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -5 + 2 = -3.\n$$\n\nSo, applying the average value formula, we find that the average value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6-1}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186105612 1074 "First, since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=-4\n$$\n\n, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= -4 \\\\\nt \\cdot 6 + (-2) \\cdot t &= -4 \\\\\n6t - 2t &= -4 \\\\\n4t &= -4 \\\\\nt &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\na = ⟨ - 1,-2⟩, b = ⟨6,-1⟩\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid a \\mid \\mid b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{x}b_{x} + a_{y}b_{y}}{\\sqrt{a_{x}^{2} + a_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{b_{x}^{2} + b_{y}^{2}}} \\\\\n&= (-4)/(\\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + (-2)^{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{6^{2} + (-1)^{2}}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5} \\cdot \\sqrt{37}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{185}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left(-\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt{185}} \\right) \\approx 107.1^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186105612 1074 "First, since $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=-4$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =-4 \\\\ t\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot t & =-4 \\\\ 6t-2t & =-4 \\\\ 4t & =-4 \\\\ t & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨-1,-2⟩,\\,b=⟨6,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{37}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{185}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt{185}} \\right) \\approx 107.1^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186105