 that the position vector of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFrom the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle$. So, the normal vector of the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= ⟨ - 1, 4, 8⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 5, 3, 2⟩ &= 33 \\\\\n- 5x + 3y + 2z &= 33 \\\\\n- 5x + 3y + 2z - 33 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119650 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =⟨-1,4,8⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z-33 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119651 1071 "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi$, find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119651 1071 "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi,$ find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119651 1074 "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y + z + 13 &= 0 \\\\\nz &= -2x + y - 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-13$. So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi$. Note that the position vector of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\n(r - ⟨0, 0,-13⟩) \\cdot ⟨2,-1, 1⟩ &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119651 1074 "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z+13 & =0 \\\\ z & =-2x+y-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0,$ we get $z=-13.$ So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119652 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119652 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119652 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= v_{1} \\cdot v_{2} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2 \\\\\n&= 1 \\mid 3 \\mid - 2 \\\\\n&=-16i - 4j - 14k \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(r - p) \\cdot n &= 0 \\\\\nr \\cdot n &= p \\cdot n \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= ⟨ - 3, 2,-6⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= 124\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨ - 16,-4,-14⟩ &= 124 \\\\\n- 16x - 4y - 14z &= 124 \\\\\n- 16x - 4y - 14z - 124 &= 0 \\\\\n8x + 2y + 7z + 62 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119652 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-16i-4j-14k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-4,-14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =⟨-3,2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ \\