the intersection point is obtained by substituting $t=1$ into the equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨5+4(1),3-2(1),-4-3(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9,1,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(9 , 1, -7).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119657 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equations\n\n$$\nl: \\mathbf{r} = \\langle 1,3,1 \\rangle + t \\langle 2,1,0 \\rangle, \\qquad  \\Pi:4x-3y+2z-7 = 0.\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119657 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equations\n$l: \\mathbf{r} = \\langle 1,3,1 \\rangle + t \\langle 2,1,0 \\rangle, \\qquad \\Pi:4x-3y+2z-7 = 0$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119657 1074 "Let's start by writing the equation of the line as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 1+2t , 3+t, 1 \\rangle .\n$$\n\nLet's also write the equation of the plane in the dot product form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3y+2z-7 & =0 \\\\ 4x-3y+2z & =7 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨4,-3,2⟩ & =7 \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,-3,2⟩ & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 1+2t , 3+t, 1 \\rangle$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨4,-3,2⟩ & =7 \\\\ ⟨1+2t,3+t,1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,-3,2⟩ & =7 \\\\ 4(1+2t)-3(3+t)+2\\cdot 1 & =7 \\\\ 4+8t-9-3t+2 & =7 \\\\ 5t & =10 \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $\\mathbf{p}$ of the intersection point $P,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨1+2(2),3+(2),1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,5,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(5 , 5, 1).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119657 1074 "Let's start by writing the equation of the line as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 1+2t, 3+t, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nLet's also write the equation of the plane in the dot product form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 3y + 2z - 7 &= 0 \\\\\n4x - 3y + 2z &= 7 \\\\\n⟨x, y, z⟩ \\cdot ⟨4,-3, 2⟩ &= 7 \\\\\nr \\cdot ⟨4,-3, 2⟩ &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 1+2t, 3+t, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\ninto the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr \\cdot ⟨4,-3, 2⟩ &= 7 \\\\\n⟨1 + 2t, 3 + t, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨4,-3, 2⟩ &= 7 \\\\\n4(1 + 2t) - 3(3 + t) + 2 \\cdot 1 &= 7 \\\\\n4 + 8t - 9 - 3t + 2 &= 7 \\\\\n5t &= 10 \\\\\nt &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $\\mathbf{p}$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=2$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= ⟨1 + 2(2), 3 + (2), 1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨5, 5, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(5, 5, 1)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119658 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equations\n![](images/29/2989739b62112c05f49beb975eb9039d1bbc7fca39ffd4d0adc058c7cf1cbb41.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119658 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equations\n${x = 3 - 4t; y =-1 + 2t; z = 2 + 3t$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119658 1074 "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 4y + 6z + 25 &= 0 \\\\\n3(3 - 4t) + 4(-1 + 2t) + 6(2 + 3t) + 25 &= 0 \\\\\n9 - 12t - 4 + 8t + 12 + 18t + 25 &= 0 \\\\\n14t &= -42 \\\\\nt &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $\\mathbf{p}$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=-3$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{x = 3 - 4(-3) = 15; y =-1 + 2(-3) =-7; z = 2 + 3(-3) =-7\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $P(15,-7,-7)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119658 1074 "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+4y+6z+25 & =0 \\\\ 3(3-4t)+4(-1+2t)+6(2+3t)+25 & =0 \\\\ 9-12t-4+8t+12+18t+25 & =0 \\\\ 14t & =-42 \\\\ t & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $\\mathbf{p}$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=-3$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-4(-3)=15 \\\\ y=-1+2(-3)=-7 \\\\ z=2+3(-3)=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $P(15,-7,-7).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119659 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,2,-2)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equation ![](images/40/40ec5f2b2615eedf6b16420a2089b7c377141db46237de35218c5e685b629918.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119659 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(5,2,-2)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119659 1074 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(4,8,2)$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ^(→) &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\mid comp_{n}PQ^(→) \\mid \\\\\n&= (\\begin{vmatrix}PQ^(→) \\cdot n &)/(\\mid n\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\\n&= (\\mid (-1) \\cdot 2 + 6 \\cdot 4 + 4 \\cdot (-4)\\begin{vmatrix})/(\\sqrt{2^{2} + 4^{2} + (-4)^{2}}) \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid - 2 + 24 - 16 \\mid}{\\sqrt{4 + 16 + 16}} \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 6\\end{vmatrix}}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119659 1074 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(4,8,2).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector ![](images/05/0589ebbdb2c14b93e1296b9e1f8b0caa0c3df49bff4c9fbb97e4c18102844ec1.png)\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-4)|}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-2+24-16|}{\\sqrt{4+16+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,6\\,|}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119660 1071 "Given that $Q(1,-2,-1)$ lies on the plane $\\Pi:\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, 0, -2 \\rangle = 4$, find the shortest distance from $P(0, -1, 2)$ to $\\Pi$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119660 1071 "Given that $Q(1,-2,-1)$ lies on the plane $\\Pi:\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, 0, -2 \\rangle = 4,$ find the shortest distance from $P(0 , -1 , 2)$ to $\\Pi.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119660 1074 "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ^(→) &= ⟨1,-2,-1⟩ - ⟨0,-1, 2⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1,-1,-3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 2, 0, -2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\mid comp_{n}PQ^(→) \\mid \\\\\n&= (\\begin{vmatrix}PQ^(→) \\cdot n &)/(\\mid n\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\\n&= (\\mid 1 \\cdot 2 + (-1) \\cdot 0 + (-3) \\cdot (-2)\\begin{vmatrix})/(\\sqrt{2^{2} + 0^{2} + (-2)^{2}}) \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 2 + 0 + 6 \\mid}{\\sqrt{4 + 0 + 4}} \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 8\\end{vmatrix}}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{8} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119660 1074 "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,-2,-1⟩-⟨0,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector $\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 2, 0, -2 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2+0+6|}{\\sqrt{4+0+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,8\\,|}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119661 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(3 , -10 , 3)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $2x+y-2z = -1$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119661 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(3, -10, 3)$ and the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $2x+y-2z = -1$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119661 1074 "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z$. Let's choose $x=4$ and $y=-5$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y - 2z &= -1 \\\\\n2(4) + (-5) + 1 &= 2z \\\\\n4 &= 2z \\\\\nz &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(4, -5, 2)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ^(→) &= ⟨4,-5, 2⟩ - ⟨3,-10, 3⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 5,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane\n\n$$\nax+by+cz=d\n$$\n\nthe normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle a,b,c \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, for the plane $\\Pi$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 2, 1, -2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\mid comp_{n}PQ^(→) \\mid \\\\\n&= (\\begin{vmatrix}PQ^(→) \\cdot n &)/(\\mid n\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\\n&= (\\mid 1 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 1 + (-1) \\cdot (-2)\\begin{vmatrix})/(\\sqrt{2^{2} + 1^{2} + (-2)^{2}}) \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 2 + 5 + 2 \\mid}{\\sqrt{4 + 1 + 4}} \\\\ = \\frac{\\mid 9\\end{vmatrix}}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119661 1074 "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=4$ and $y=-5$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z & =-1 \\\\ 2(4)+(-5)+1 & =2z \\\\ 4 & =2z \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(4, -5, 2)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overrightarrow{PQ},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨4,-5,2⟩-⟨3,-10,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,5,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle a,b,c \\rangle.$ So, for the plane $\\Pi,$ we have $\\mathbf{n}= \\langle 2, 1, -2 \\rangle.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\\Pi$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2+5+2|}{\\sqrt{4+1+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,9\\,|}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119662 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n![](images/4b/4b3aa22ba1342c45d422c227258837ae080ee705f2580437c775bd2beaf8afeb.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119662 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119662 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ca/cafe7ac2b6224a50a288cabf6b6838c6a85a722404c9ebd20238f689f196b841.png)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}, \\; y=2\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119662 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y-2}{0}\n$$\n\ninvolves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 4t \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 + 5t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y-2}{0}\n$$\n\npart and instead write $y=2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}, \\; y=2\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119663 1071 "Using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th row, write the following determinant as a combination of $3 \\times 3$ determinants:\n\n![](images/50/502b9464378fb5030a72918f45938184f120773e4639f3f50f071ca82fc307ce.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119663 1071 "Using a cofactor expansion along the $4$th row, write the following determinant as a combination of $3 \\times 3$ determinants:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119663 1074 "Using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} &= (-6) \\cdot C_{41} + 0 \\cdot C_{42} + 0 \\cdot C_{43} + 1 \\cdot C_{44} \\\\\n&= (-6) \\cdot (-1)^{4 + 1} \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+ 1 \\cdot (-1)^{4 + 4} \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119663 1074 "Using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =(-6)\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+1\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/a1/a1c11cfd7d1cacae0881118776eb6b38dea7b92c4f542e8158e31bbfa59274ec.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119664 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=2 \\\\ 2x+y=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as\n![](images/2d/2d3571405367c6d4ce15927a1dbdb50a30af7d13580c7b9cd3c73bada0583c98.png)\nExpress $D_x$ in terms of a $2 \\times 2$ determinant." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119664 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n${x - y = 2; 2x + y = 1$.\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}$\nExpress $D_x$ in terms of a $2 \\times 2$ determinant." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119664 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=2 \\\\ 2x+y=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct $D_x$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119664 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{x - y = 2; 2x + y = 1\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD &= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct $D_x$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{x} &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119665 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n![](images/c0/c0a1d1860f5be98c6606f191fd26b0f799bc182b7b4e7fdca542d693290f11e4.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119665 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119665 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form. We see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $2$nd columns. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* two vectors. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/26/268ea5cffffb0765f5c4bbacff7f3253ad4243e0e1ffa77d3a8a5a7f794d62ba.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119665 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form. We see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $2$nd columns. This tells us that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}\n$$\n\nforms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* two vectors. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119666 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n![](images/66/668c8e17567f5635f3d39c98dced5c77c60a287cac8a92d29db2e8afefe94cec.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119666 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119666 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has one pivot columns: the $1$st column. This tells us that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{v}_1 \\}\n$$\n\nforms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of one vector, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* one vector. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119666 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has one pivot columns: the $1$st column. This tells us that $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1 \\}$ forms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of one vector, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* one vector. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/41/4196e42d01f879b24974be9c0d1bc456c4894cb19b832efab46f3cdb8a28b644.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119667 1071 "Let $A$ be a $4 \\times 7$ matrix that has $3$ **non-pivot** columns. Find $\\textrm{rank}(A)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119667 1071 "Let $A$ be a $4 \\times 7$ matrix that has $3$ **non-pivot** columns. Find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119667 1074 "We know that $\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A$.\n\nSince $A$ has $7$ columns and $3$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is\n$7-3=4$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A)=4\n$$\n\n." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119667 1074 "We know that $\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A$. \n\nSince $A$ has $7$ columns and $3$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n7-3=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119668 1071 "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $4$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119668 1071 "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $4?$\n\n![](images/1e/1e19a98b8ff5b436e74552c814581f016d4182e6fc552ee69273cd70bbe2f9de.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119668 1074 "First, notice that a matrix of rank $4$ must have at least $4$ columns and at least $4$ rows.\nSo, we disregard the matrix $B$ since it has only $3$ rows.\n\nAlso, the matrix must have $4$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $A$ has only $3$ pivot columns, while the matrix $C$ has exactly $4$ pivot columns. So,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A)=3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(C)=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119668 1074 "First, notice that a matrix of rank $4$ must have at least $4$ columns and at least $4$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the matrix $B$ since it has only $3$ rows. \n\nAlso, the matrix must have $4$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $A$ has only $3$ pivot columns, while the matrix $C$ has exactly $4$ pivot columns. So, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3$ and $\\textrm{rank}(C)=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119669 1071 "If ![](images/fe/fe24ddff89c73a9a50b4b489ab3828763b68685e158a82041b1ce2be59f854d6.png), find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186119669 1071 "If $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$, find $\\textrm{rank}(A)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119669 1074 "To find the rank of $A$, we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + 2R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 6R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 35\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $3$ pivot columns (or, alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A)=3\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186119669 1074 "To find the rank of $A,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 35\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $3$ pivot columns (or, alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124303 1071 "Let $L$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. For any $p(x),q(x) \\in L$, we have $p(x)+q(x) = q(x)+p(x)$.\n2. For any $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L$, we have $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x) = p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)]$.\n3. For each $p(x) \\in L$, we have $-p(x) \\in L$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124303 1071 "Let $L$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is less than or equal to $2.$  Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- For any $p(x),q(x) \\in L,$ we have $p(x)+q(x) = q(x)+p(x).$\n\n- For any $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we have $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x) = p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$\n\n- For each $p(x) \\in L,$ we have $-p(x) \\in L$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124303 1074 "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x), q(x) \\in L$, we obtain that\n$p(x) + q(x)= q(x) +p(x)$\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L$, we obtain that\n$[p(x) + q(x)] + r(x) = p(x) + [q(x) + r(x)]$\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x) \\in L$, there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n$p(x) + (-p(x))= p(x) - p(x) = 0$.\nNotice that\n$\\deg(-p(x)) = \\deg(p(x)) \\leq 2$,\nhence $-p(x) \\in L$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124303 1074 "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x), q(x) \\in L,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(x) + q(x)= q(x) +p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x) \\in L,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x) + q(x)] + r(x) = p(x) + [q(x) + r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x) \\in L,$ there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n\n$$\np(x) + (-p(x))= p(x) - p(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\deg(-p(x)) = \\deg(p(x)) \\leq 2,\n$$\n\nhence $-p(x) \\in L.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124304 1071 "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\in V$. Given that $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\{4\\mathbf{v}_1, 5\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly independent\n2. $\\{\\mathbf{0}, \\mathbf{v}_1, 2\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ is linearly independent\n3. $\\{\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124304 1071 "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\in V.$ Given that $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\{ 4\\mathbf{v}_1, 5\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly independent\n\n- $\\{ \\mathbf{0}, \\mathbf{v}_1, 2\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ is linearly independent\n\n- $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124304 1074 "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(4{v}_{1})+{x}_{2}(5{v}_{2})+{x}_{3}{v}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}_{1}){v}_{1}+(5{x}_{2}){v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is $x_1=x_2=x_3=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n\\{ 4\\mathbf{v}_1, 5\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + 0 \\cdot (2\\mathbf{v}_2) = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, $x_1\\mathbf{0}+x_2\\mathbf{v}_1+x_3(2\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{ \\mathbf{0}, \\mathbf{v}_1, 2\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $x_1\\mathbf{v}_2+x_2\\mathbf{v}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ must have only the zero solution $x_1=x_2=0.$  If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + b \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_3=\\mathbf{0},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + a \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + b \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124304 1074 "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n$x_{1}(4v_{1}) + x_{2}(5v_{2}) + x_{3}v_{3}|= 0$,\nwhich, in turn, gives\n$(4x_{1})v_{1} + (5x_{2})v_{2} + x_{3}v_{3}|= 0$.\nSince $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n${4x_{1} = 0; 5x_{2} = 0; x_{3} = 0$.\nThe unique solution of the system is $x_1=x_2=x_3=0$, which means that $\\{4\\mathbf{v}_1, 5\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly independent.\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, notice that\n$1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + 0 \\cdot (2\\mathbf{v}_2) = \\mathbf{0}$.\nAs a result, $x_1\\mathbf{0}+x_2\\mathbf{v}_1+x_3(2\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{\\mathbf{0}, \\mathbf{v}_1, 2\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $x_1\\mathbf{v}_2+x_2\\mathbf{v}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ must have only the zero solution $x_1=x_2=0$. If thi