ement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nis true if and only if $x_1=x_2=0$. So, the set is linearly independent.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry $a_{11} = 0$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124309 1071 "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2},- \\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n\n- $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0}  \\right\\}$ spans $V$\n\n- $\\left\\{  -\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124309 1071 "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2},-\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ is a basis of $V$\n2. $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ spans $V$\n3. $\\left\\{-\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124309 1074 "Since $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V$, and\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n$x_{1}b_{2} + x_{2}(-b_{1}) + x_{3}(b_{3} + b_{1})|= 0$\nis equivalent to\n$x_{1}b_{2} + (x_{3} - x_{2})b_{1} + x_{3}b_{3}|= 0$,\nwhich, since $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n${x_{1} = 0; x_{3} - x_{2} = 0; x_{3} = 0$\nAlso, $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2},-\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2},-\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1} \\right\\}$:\n$x_1\\mathbf{b}_1+x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+x_3\\mathbf{b}_3 = x_2\\mathbf{b}_2+(x_3-x_1)(-\\mathbf{b}_1)+x_3 (\\mathbf{b_3}+\\mathbf{b_1})$\n- Statement II is false. In order for $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ to span $V$, every element of $\\mathcal{B}$ must be able to be expressed as a linear combination of elements of $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$. But the vector $\\mathbf{b}_1 \\in \\mathcal{B}$ can't be written as a linear combination of the elements of $\\left\\{\\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$.\n- Statement III is false. If $\\left\\{-\\mathbf{b_2}, 3\\mathbf{b_1}\\right\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist $x_1$ and $x_2$ not both zero such that\n$x_1(-\\mathbf{b_2}) + x_2(3\\mathbf{b_1}) = \\mathbf{0}$.\nThen, we would have\n$(3x_2) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_1} + (-x_1) \\cdot \\mathbf{b_2} + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{b}_3= \\mathbf{0}$.\nBut this would mean that $\\mathcal{B} = \\left\\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent, which contradicts the fact that $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis of $V$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124309 1074 "Since $\\mathcal{B}$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Stateme