aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)(-4)-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $\\mathbf{x} = 2 \\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain\n![](images/88/883431c76485c9fd706fe79c2bf3936946cfb0dba602ebd0df77d6cc9e800665.png)\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124316 1071 "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124316 1071 "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}.$\n\n![](images/0d/0dcc93449ac1d14e51850e4a74f639aeb3acd21b720ba2b9deeb2208d9fffeb4.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124316 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{S}}\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$. As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{S}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$. To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{S}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S → B} &= P_{B → S}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 \\cdot 10 - 3 \\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[x]_{B} &= P_{S → B}[x]_{S} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124316 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{S}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ As a result, we have\n\n![](images/50/50bbd2dd0fa1c0732a01d07c2614ab15549272fcc0b7d686651f0464b3b50f39.png)\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{S}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{S}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 10-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124317 1071 "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {(x-2)^2} {2} +  {y^2} + \\dfrac{(z+1)^2}{2} =1,$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124317 1071 "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {(x-2)^2} {2} + {y^2} + \\dfrac{(z+1)^2}{2} =1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124317 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - 2)^{2})/(2) + (0)^{2} + ((0 + 1)^{2})/(2) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(2) + \\frac{1}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 2)^{2})/(2) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n(x - 2)^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx - 2 &= ± 1 \\\\\nx &= 2 ± 1 \\\\\nx &= 1, 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are\n\n$$\n\\left(1, 0, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 0, 0\\right)\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124317 1074 "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+(0)^{2}+\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =1,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $\\left(1, 0, 0\\right)$ and $\\left(3, 0, 0\\right).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124318 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $(x-1)^2 + y^2 - \\dfrac{z^2}{4} = -1$, find the domain of $z = z(x,y)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124318 1071 "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $(x-1)^2 + y^2 - \\dfrac{z^2}{4} = -1,$ find the domain of $z = z(x,y).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124318 1074 "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} - \\frac{z^{2}}{4} &= -1 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} + 1 &= \\frac{z^{2}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{z^{2}}{4} &= (x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} + 1 \\\\\nz^{2} &= 4((x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} + 1) \\\\\nz &= ± 2\\sqrt{(x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2$, we have\n\n$$\n(x-1)^2 + y^2 + 1 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is $\\mathbb R^2$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124318 1074 "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+1 & =\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =4((x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x-1)^2 + y^2 + 1 \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is $\\mathbb R^2.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124319 1071 "Find the vertex and the axis of symmetry of the elliptic paraboloid $z= 4x^2 + 8(y + 2)^2$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124319 1071 "Find the vertex and the axis of symmetry of the elliptic paraboloid $z= 4x^2 + 8(y + 2)^2.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124319 1074 "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is \n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-0 & =4{x}^{2}+8(y+2)^{2} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{1/8} \\\\ z-0 & =\\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{1/4}+\\frac{(y-(-2))^{2}}{1/8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,-2,0).$  Since it is oriented along the $z$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124319 1074 "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid with vertex at a point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz - 0 &= 4x^{2} + 8(y + 2)^{2} \\\\\nz - 0 &= \\frac{x^{2}}{1/4} + ((y + 2)^{2})/(1/8) \\\\\nz - 0 &= ((x - 0)^{2})/(1/4) + ((y - (-2))^{2})/(1/8)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid that has its vertex at $(0,-2,0)$. Also, from the equation, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{x = 0; y =-2\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124320 1071 "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+1=\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4} - (y-2) ^2$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124320 1071 "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+1=\\dfrac{(x-1)^2}{4} - (y-2) ^2.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124320 1074 "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz + 1 &= ((x - 1)^{2})/(4) - (y - 2)^{2} \\\\\nz - (-1) &= ((x - 1)^{2})/(4) - (y - 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(1,2,-1)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124320 1074 "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}-(y-2)^{2} \\\\ z-(-1) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{4}-(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(1,2,-1).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124321 1071 "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere $(x+1)^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 8z = 20$?" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124321 1071 "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere $(x+1)^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 8z = 20?$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124321 1074 "The equation of a sphere centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ with radius $r > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-x_0)^2 + (y-y_0)^2 + (z-z_0)^2 = r^2\n$$\n\nBy completing the square for the variable $z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} + 8z &= 20 \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} + y^{2} + (z^{2} + 8z + 16) &= 20 + 16 \\\\\n(x + 1)^{2} + y^{2} + (z + 4)^{2} &= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our equation as\n\n$$\n(x-(-1))^2 + (y-0)^2 + (z-(-4))^2 = 6^2\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the center at the point $C(-1,0,-4)$ and the radius $r=6$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124321 1074 "The equation of a sphere centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ with radius $r > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-x_0)^2 + (y-y_0)^2 + (z-z_0)^2 = r^2.\n$$\n\nBy completing the square for the variable $z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+8z & =20 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+({z}^{2}+8z+16) & =20+16 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z+4)^{2} & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our equation as\n\n$$\n(x-(-1))^2 + (y-0)^2 + (z-(-4))^2 = 6^2,\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the center at the point $C(-1,0,-4)$ and the radius $r=6.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124322 1071 "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the sphere $x^2 + (y+2)^2 + (z-3)^2 = 25$?" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124322 1071 "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the sphere $x^2 + (y+2)^2 + (z-3)^2 = 25?$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124322 1074 "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} + (0 - 3)^{2} &= 25 \\\\\nx^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} + 9 &= 25 \\\\\nx^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of the circle of radius $4$ centered at $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124322 1074 "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}+(0-3)^{2} & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}+9 & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of the circle of radius $4$ centered at $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124323 1071 "If $A$ is a $9 \\times 11$ matrix that has $9$ pivot columns, then what is $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$?" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124323 1071 "If $A$ is a $9 \\times 11$ matrix that has $9$ pivot columns, then what is $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))?$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124323 1074 "We know that $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$ is the number of non-pivot columns in $A$.\n\nSince $A$ has $11$ columns and $9$ of them are pivots, then the number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is\n$11-9=2$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=2\n$$\n\n." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124323 1074 "We know that $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$ is the number of non-pivot columns in $A$. \n\nSince $A$ has $11$ columns and $9$ of them are pivots, then the number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n11-9=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=2$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124324 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If ![](images/62/62cc7238e6676b8a43d216ab6c8b30504602c68fd548832eceac9b710b885471.png) then $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=2$\n\n- If ![](images/b8/b85d717afa8af39d10517f909a09b53e6d379beef6e04e3d9ce7d3e48c88dca4.png) then $\\textrm{dim(Null}(B))=0$\n\n- If ![](images/8e/8e12a5386c3db2149324bd28c174a5410f7751e0c963cf80969e0cfdf14e5d7b.png) then $\\textrm{dim(Null}(C))=1$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124324 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=2$\n2. If $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\textrm{dim(Null}(B))=0$\n3. If $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ then $\\textrm{dim(Null}(C))=1$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124324 1074 "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix.\n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The matrix $A$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $2$nd column). So, $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=1$.\n- Statement II is false. The matrix $B$ has $1$ pivot column and $2$ non-pivot columns (namely, the $2$nd and $3$rd columns). So, $\\textrm{dim(Null}(B))=2$.\n- Statement III is true. The matrix $C$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$nd column). So, $\\textrm{dim(Null}(C))=1$.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124324 1074 "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The matrix $A$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $2$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The matrix $B$ has $1$ pivot column and $2$ non-pivot columns (namely, the $2$nd and $3$rd columns). So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(B))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The matrix $C$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(C))=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124325 1071 "Given ![](images/e2/e20df1b44d2bfe100499eebfb4d2bdf8c1055978fdccbe11c8b5c0882052ace8.png) what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124325 1071 "Given $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)$?" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124325 1074 "To find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(A))$, we reduce $A$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{4} \\mid:=R_{4} + 3R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$rd column).\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(A)) = 1\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124325 1074 "To find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(A))$, we reduce $A$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$rd column). \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(A)) = 1.\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124326 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 2x_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -x_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n1. $A$ is not invertible\n2. $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^4$\n3. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124326 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, and \n![](images/ac/ac61fea55da2b0f5b4c2b21b8915c7eb7fc30c576b2ca7dea830ae0aa9183dd6.png)\n\n- $A$ is not invertible\n\n- $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^4$\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124326 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nCol(A) = R^{4}; dim(Col(A)) = 4 = rank(A); Null(A) = {0}; dim(Null(A)) = 0 = nullity(A)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nwe conclude that\n$\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=1 \\neq 0$.\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n- Statement II is false. Since the matrix $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Col}(A) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^4$.\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{rank}(A) \\neq 4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124326 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following: \n\n![](images/f5/f57560debe62c432de44b86e2b1f569b703b9ef3faae46bcc2df18cca17a2c62.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since \n![](images/65/65f646a200575b7299fc0278f7a5dc73cba98e293943bdb592ca24c4dd01df0f.png)\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=1 \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nSo,  according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the matrix $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Col}(A) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{rank}(A) \\neq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124327 1071 "Find the standard matrix that represents  the  linear  transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where\n![](images/c3/c3c56ecc3e59befc020d3fb3c636c5831314b05d420dc89fb6d0e1c46a22fd25.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124327 1071 "Find the standard matrix that represents the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124327 1074 "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix ${T}$ such that\n    ![](images/4e/4e3cee63e90fa235c9b43ca40c4e7dbe854ec75221482000288e3944cf9ce6ad.png)\n    Notice that\n    ![](images/9b/9bd6fc63a6e7a864954ad754c0467857aec0ab1e288755a286633b657d369072.png)\n\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n    ![](images/0b/0b8af627003945f3d329d6887260b217ce8d72fea1c8fdd9dcc92c2db26a3992.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124327 1074 "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix ${T}$ such that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\nNotice that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124328 1071 "Find the standard matrix that represents  the  linear  transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where\n![](images/55/55441af96891ed4e0b91bdc6415d5f671ab0ebd39fcfd69a121162b423c194cd.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124328 1071 "Find the standard matrix that represents the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124328 1074 "First, rewrite the order of the variables in the usual way:\n\n![](images/0d/0d6728cdb8030be1cfcd2c819ef363032bf18ba80fd6bebe157f4022839e5d9a.png)\n\nWe require a $2\\times 2$ matrix ${T}$ such that\n    ![](images/8f/8f93d75dddcbee8927cb8baf990caaff8d1db2e875b497b17ef930bc7e97486c.png)\n\nNotice that\n    ![](images/6a/6a06c4ed3e01b53c334a640f492b405a66b9ff54e20c828f1d0a4c748051d5b0.png)\n\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n    ![](images/6c/6c4fc4d94830c32fa3b9c6c3371604fa398e7d701031114914de3b4a31714cd7.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124328 1074 "First, rewrite the order of the variables in the usual way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe require a $2\\times 2$ matrix ${T}$ such that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124329 1071 "What is the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ whose standard matrix is ![](images/4a/4a4616061123e27093fa73d17de5c81fb68527b83993cba4eec22fc0368fe364.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124329 1071 "What is the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ whose standard matrix is $\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124329 1074 "Consider a general vector ![](images/c5/c5e95c3c6c86e0719be77fc3db865c7a81995ef386ce7508ccef19ab307a783a.png) Multiplying by the matrix $T,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8x-y \\\\ -5x-2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,$ is given by\n\n![](images/e6/e6c0ddd895406c6b79e4c520a7a4002060c917b51a6070f134be8c47dd63de0b.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124329 1074 "Consider a general vector $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ Multiplying by the matrix $T$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}8 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8x - y \\\\ -5x - 2y\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124330 1071 "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ given below. If $T$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, find $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124330 1071 "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ given belo