 the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y$. That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=-2y$.\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124331 1074 "Finding the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is ![](images/5f/5fa2e7ff6cfc27fce7698124fb835abf5c6f02221ba7c7ebb0b371fef6d3c15a.png)\n\nSetting ![](images/f9/f9fc80048a348773c656ec98ac9299eb1c611bfb07eee747b883eb7398dc295f.png) we then need to solve the equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is ![](images/ca/cace77f13c7ef817cfefaab742bdca008b29a177b0783dc761bc24d8a1273af3.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124332 1071 "Given the linear transformation ![](images/a4/a441d65e864253e32c59c58b6b0684482b6eaee7fd443fdb2f7c026f0fe10d1f.png) find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124332 1071 "Given the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124332 1074 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{nullity}(T) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))\n$$\n\nIn order to find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))$, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 3R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot columns. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))=0\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124332 1074 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/f2/f27d3195556ff8786f9416cddbe4ad52cf1838f2b0ce37b4da11746fee59fb9c.png)\n\nRecall that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{nullity}(T) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot columns. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(T))=0.\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124333 1071 "Consider the linear transformation ![](images/d0/d0a0fabd7275cdceed8c0c62e58bbfc0e5f23322145d551e412877075cbb6e3c.png) and the vector ![](images/4d/4dd5f4c300c5870e696c5485fffdef1bbd9b7741b23c7d27fda1198e31baa33b.png) Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,\\mathbf{u}\\in\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124333 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,\\mathbf{u}\\in\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124333 1074 "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(u) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot k + 12 \\cdot 2 - 3 \\cdot 6 \\\\ -2 \\cdot k + 4 \\cdot 2 - 2 \\cdot 6 \\\\ 6 \\cdot k - 3 \\cdot 2 + 3 \\cdot 6\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}3k + 6 \\\\ -2k - 4 \\\\ 6k + 12\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{3k + 6 = 0; - 2k - 4 = 0; 6k + 12 = 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124333 1074 "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nSo, we need to solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(u) & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot k+12\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 6 \\\\ -2\\cdot k+4\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 6 \\\\ 6\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3k+6 \\\\ -2k-4 \\\\ 6k+12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3k+6=0 \\\\ -2k-4=0 \\\\ 6k+12=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to all three of these equations is $k=-2.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124334 1071 "The standard matrix $T$ 