nd{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124359 1071 "There are two groups of people in a particular city: those who use their own vehicles and those who use public transport. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport, and\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles.\n\nGive the stochastic matrix that describes how people change their means of transportation each year." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124359 1071 "There are two groups of people in a particular city: those who use their own vehicles and those who use public transport. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport, and\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles.\n\nGive the stochastic matrix that describes how people change their means of transportation each year." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124359 1074 "We are given the following situation for each year:\n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Vehicles** | **Public Transport** |\n| To: | **Vehicles** | $0.65$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Public Transport** | $0.35$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0.65 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.75\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124359 1074 "We are given the following situation for each year: \n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Vehicles** | **Public Transport ** |\n| To: | **Vehicles** | $0.65$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Public Transport** | $0.35$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n![](images/b2/b21fee1abe56dcfdc687be14278c62a20d201ff00e96f1e5918ce22d9dc8b03c.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124360 1071 "A certain industry produces $2$ types of sugar, namely sugar $A$ and sugar $B$. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of sugar in a month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Sugar $A$** | **Sugar $B$** |\n| To: | **Sugar $A$** | $0.3$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sugar $B$** | $0.7$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nThat is, $70\\%$ of sugar $A$ customers switch to sugar $B$ each month, and $45\\%$ of sugar $B$ customers switch to sugar $A.$\n\nCurrently, $600$ customers use sugar $A,$ while $400$ customers use sugar $B.$ How many customers will use sugar $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124360 1071 "A certain industry produces $2$ types of sugar, namely sugar $A$ and sugar $B$. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of sugar in a month:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **Sugar $A$** | **Sugar $B$** |\n| To: | **Sugar $A$** | $0.3$ | $0.45$ |\n| **Sugar $B$** | $0.7$ | $0.55$ |  |\n\nThat is, $70\\%$ of sugar $A$ customers switch to sugar $B$ each month, and $45\\%$ of sugar $B$ customers switch to sugar $A$.\n\nCurrently, $600$ customers use sugar $A$, while $400$ customers use sugar $B$. How many customers will use sugar $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124360 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.55\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of sugar is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}600 \\\\ 400\\end{bmatrix}$\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000)$, we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}600 \\\\ 400\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_1$ after one month, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1=P\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= Px_{0} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.55\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.36 \\\\ 0.64\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $\\color{blue}36\\%$ of customers will use sugar $A$. Computing $36\\%$ of $1\\,000$, we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000 \\cdot 0.36 = 360\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360$ customers will use sugar $A$ in one month." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124360 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n![](images/45/451b82e8cb00ede7b0ddf73f35c0555d0eeeeefc5beb5ce4fddd54f0df5f2577.png)\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of sugar is\n![](images/56/56952aeed1d718033af8a3e6d09e8e269f1d70aa8dc31609b8289f025f2e6bd6.png)\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n![](images/ca/cae1bed6b8b01b3ff66c2e8bacf7a10f6c0ece53f3aeac0fb1a51e8fc3c07e8e.png)\n\nTo find the state vector $\\mathbf{x}_1$ after one month, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1=P\\mathbf{x}_0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.55\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.36 \\\\ 0.64\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $\\color{blue}36\\%$ of customers will use sugar $A.$ Computing $36\\%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000 \\cdot 0.36 = 360.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $360$ customers will use sugar $A$ in one month." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124361 1071 "Consider the linear transformation\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\nand the vectors\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}$?" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124361 1071 "Consider the linear transformation \n![](images/c3/c3d64ddcfd33db2e96caf6db5d85587857c3715e8d91a7dc8f4801c117fc89b0.png) \nand the vectors \n![](images/4f/4fbbb82838d7fa94fe0aeb47ed48952bfe573e12874d3891f8650a15b46e0ee7.png)\nWhich of the given vectors belong to the kernel of $\\mathbf{T}?$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124361 1074 "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we need to compute $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_i)$ for each vector $\\mathbf v_i$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(v_{1}) &= T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(v_{2}) &= T(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(v_{3}) &= T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\,\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that, of the given vectors, the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\,\\mathbf{v}_1$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124361 1074 "A vector $\\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ if $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_i)$ for each vector $\\mathbf v_i\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)=\\mathbf{0},$ we conclude that, of the given vectors, the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,\\mathbf{T}$ is $\\,\\mathbf{v}_1.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124362 1071 "Let ![](images/1c/1c8969401a7c812e945fdc0b2f1774f6cd1d1d9c35269b2b20ea8165449d427f.png) be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector ![](images/05/05f7384dd957b2616433104bfefbb8b3e7aaa619dbb1dff94fec43c8a99e6c3c.png) belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186124362 1071 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186124362 1074 "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ if there exists a vector ![](images/be/be82bf02cdfe6a50557b312ba78708463248f844f81016df2cda41c6cfbb6c1a.png) such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ -x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y=4 \\\\ -x+y=k \\\\ 2y=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $y=4,$ which we su