al to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -kt.\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186128941 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n> *The **volume** of water $V(t)$ in a leaking tank **decreases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **time** $t$ (in minutes) since the leak started.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume* of water varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$.\n- We're told that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t}$ is *proportional to* the *time* since the water begins to leak. In other words,\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} \\propto t$.\nRecall that $\\propto$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the volume of water *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm d t} = -kt$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186128944 1071 "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the matrix $A$ are given below.\nIf $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=3$, then what is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186128944 1071 "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the matrix $A$ are given below. \nIf $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=3,$ then what is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}?$\n\n![](images/5f/5f1bfa33d135d95e3153e3305da50b69541d88d1cd78b5b6ad030a89861f3e10.pn