33388 17592186045585 false] [17592186133592 1074 "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}29 & =|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {‖u{‖}^{2}\\cdot ‖v‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =4‖v{‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4‖v{‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} & =4‖v{‖}^{2}+5(‖v{‖}^{2}+‖w{‖}^{2}) \\\\  & \\le 4‖v{‖}^{2}+5(‖v{‖}^{2}+{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =9‖v{‖}^{2}+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}29 & \\le 9‖v{‖}^{2}+20 \\\\ 9 & \\le 9‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 1 & \\le ‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $1 \\leq \\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert^2$ implies that $1 \\leq \\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert$. Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert$ is $1.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186133593 1071 "For the vector ![](images/b8/b85705767814eae0f99345888b1f0c65a89dbbc76458d7093a02a20937d2e1c3.png) which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set ![](images/85/85cd8d0ebcd70de93ec2bd5007907a02d81daadd27b348e7c72ccef53654d881.png)\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set ![](images/e0/e03fd2577b8d7067f4507d0ee6fe732d3786a494134cd967d3c65de01e28e709.png)" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186133593 1071 "For the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186133593 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of