388 17592186045585 false] [17592186171816 1071 "Determine the value of $\\displaystyle{5 \\choose 4}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186171816 1074 "The binomial coefficient $\\displaystyle{n \\choose r}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{n \\choose r} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,  r!}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $n = 5$ and $r = 4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5}{4}) & =\\frac{5!}{(5-4)!\\cdot 4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 4!}{1!\\cdot 4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{1\\cdot \\cancel{4!}} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186171816 1074 "The binomial coefficient $\\displaystyle{n \\choose r}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{n \\choose r} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\, r!}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $n = 5$ and $r = 4$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{5}{4}) &= (5!)/((5 - 4)! \\cdot 4!) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5 \\cdot 4!}{1! \\cdot 4!} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5 \\cdot 4!}{1 \\cdot 4!} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186171817 1074 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^2 = 4p(x-h)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $y$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} - 8x - 6y + 33 &= 0 \\\\\ny^{2} - 6y &= 8x - 33 \\\\\n(y - 3)^{2} - 9 &= 8x - 33 \\\\\n(y - 3)^{2} &= 8x - 33 + 9 \\\\\n(y - 3)^{2} &= 8x - 24 \\\\\n(y - 3)^{2} &= 8(x - 3) \\\\\n(y - 3)^{2} &= 4(2)(x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (3,3)\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186171817 1074 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^2 = 4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $y$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-8x-6y+33 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-6y & =8x-33 \\\\ (y-3)^{2}-9 & =8x-33 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =8x-33+9 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =8x-24 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =8(x-3) \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =4(2)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k) = (3,3).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186175785 1071 "Given the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=3,\n$$\n\nuse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(2)$ using two steps of equal size." 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186175785 1071 "Given the initial value problem\n$y' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=3$,\nuse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(2)$ using two steps of equal size." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186175785 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2)$. The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1$, and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=3$.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2xy = 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 3 = 6 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = 3 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = 1 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 3 + 3 = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y' \\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2xy = 2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 6 = 18 \\\\\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx = 18 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = 9 \\\\\nx_{new} &= x + Δx = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = 2 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy = 6 + 9 = 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $18$ | $9$ |\n| $2$ | $15$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx 15\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186175785 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=3.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+3=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y' \\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 6=18 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=9 