 3\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of our quartic equation are\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2\\textrm{i}, \\, \\pm 3 \\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186175794 1074 "First, we let $f(x) = x^4 + 13 x^2 + 36.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and $x_1=2\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overline{x_1}=\\overline{2\\textrm{i}}=-2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$\\big(x-2\\text{i}\\big)\\big(x-(-2\\text{i})\\big)$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2\\text{i})(x-(-2\\text{i})) & =(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2+4$ using long division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+9 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}+13{x}^{2}+36\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,9{x}^{2}+36 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{9{x}^{2}+36}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x) = (x^2+4)(x^2+9).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve $x^2+9 = 0{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=\\pm 2\\textrm{i}, \\, \\pm 3 \\textrm{i}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186179520 1071 "Given that $n \\geq 2$ is an integer, simplify $\\dfrac {n!} {(n - 2)!}$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186179520 1071 "Given that $n \\geq 2$ is an integer, simplify $\\dfrac {n!} {(n - 2)!}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186179520 1074 "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\nn! = n \\cdot (n-1) \\cdot (n-2)\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(n-2)$! that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {n!} {(n - 2)!} &= \\dfrac {n \\cdot (n - 1) \\cdot (n - 2)!} {(n - 2)!} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {n \\cdot (n - 1) \\cdot \\cancel{(n - 2)!}} {\\cancel{(n - 2)!}} \\\\[5pt] &= n(n -1) \\\\[5pt] &= n^2 - n \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186179520 1074 "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\nn! = n \\cdot (n-1) \\cdot (n-2)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(n-2)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {n!} {(n - 2)!} \n&= \\dfrac {n \\cdot (n - 1) \\cdot (n - 2)!} {(n - 2)!}\\[5pt] \n\n&= \\dfrac {n \\cdot (n - 1) \\cdot \\cancel{(n - 2)!}} {\\cancel{(n - 2)!}}\\[5pt] \n\n&= n(n -1) \\[5pt]\n&= n^2 - n\n\t}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186179521 1071 "Given that $k \\geq 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\dfrac {k^2(k - 1)!} {k!}$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186179521 1071 "Given that $k \\geq 1$ is an integer, simplify $\\dfrac {k^2(k - 1)!} {k!}.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186179521 1074 "First, we notice that\n\n$$\nk! = k \\cdot (k-1)\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify the numerator as follows:\n\n$$\nk^2 (k-1)! = k \\cdot \\underbrace{k \\cdot (k-1)!}_{k!} = k \\cdot k\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $k$! that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {k^2(k - 1)!} {k!} &= \\dfrac {k \\cdot k!} {k!} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {k \\cdot \\cancel{k!}} {\\cancel{k!}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {k} {1} \\\\[5pt] &= k \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186179521 1074 "First, we notice that \n\n$$\nk! = k \\cdot (k-1)!\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify the numerator as follows:\n\n$$\nk^2 (k-1)! = k \\cdot \\underbrace{k \\cdot (k-1)!}_{k!} = k \\cdot k!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $k!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {k^2(k - 1)!} {k!} \n&= \\dfrac {k \\cdot k!} {k!}\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {k \\cdot \\cancel{k!}} {\\cancel{k!}} \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac {k} {1} \\[5pt]\n&= k\n\t}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186184071 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\textrm A1] - [\\textrm A8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*If $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector such that $\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{w}$, then $\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{0}.$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (w+w)+(-w) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{add [math]-\\mathbf{w}[/math] to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(w+(-w)) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186184071 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\textrm A1] - [\\textrm A8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n> *If $\\mathbf{w}$ is a vector such that $\\mathbf{w}+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{w}$, then $\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{0}$.*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}w + w|= w & [given] \\\\ (w + w) + (-w)|= w + (-w) & [add [math]-\\mathbf{w}[/math] to both sides] \\\\ w + (w + (-w))|= w + (-w) & [axiom __] \\\\ w + 0|= 0 & [axiom A4] \\\\ w|= 0 & [axiom __]\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe axioms:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}[A1]: x + y = y + x & [A5]: 1 \\cdot x = x \\\\ [A2]: (x + y) + z = x + (y + z) & [A6]: α(βx) = (αβ)x \\\\ [A3]: x + 0 = x & [A7]: (α + β)x = αx + βx \\\\ [A4]: x + (-x) = 0 & [A8]: α(x + y) = αx + αy\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186184071 1074 "Using the axioms of a vector space, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}w + w &= w & [given] \\\\ (w + w) + (-w)|= w + (-w) & [add [math]-\\mathbf{w}[/math] to both sides] \\\\ w + (w + (-w))|= w + (-w) & [axiom [math]{\\color{blue}\\text{A2}}[/math]] \\\\ w + 0|= 0 & [axiom A4] \\\\ w|= 0 & [axiom [math]{\\color{blue}\\text{A3}}[/math]]\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186184071 1074 "Using the axioms of a vector space, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (w+w)+(-w) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{add [math]-\\mathbf{w}[/math] to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(w+(-w)) & =w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom [math]{\\color{blue}\\text{A2}}[/math]}\\,] \\\\ w+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom [math]{\\color{blue}\\text{A3}}[/math]}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186184072 1071 "Find the solution of $2x^2-6 \\le -4x$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186184072 1071 "Find the solution of $2x^2-6 \\le -4x.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186184072 1074 "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x^{2} - 6 & \\le - 4x \\\\ 2x^{2} + 4x - 6 & \\le 0 \\\\ x^{2} + 2x - 3 & \\le 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola\n\n$$\ny=x^2+2x-3\n$$\n\nlies below the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(x + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=1$ and $x=-3$, and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/0f/0fe5ab0d38cea340e2227157bf1d434a0fccbab153289db52106857530fbc4dc.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving\n\n$$\nx^2+2x-3 \\le 0\n$$\n\nwe look for the part of the parabola that is below the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n\n$$\n-3 \\le x \\le 1\n$$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x \\in [-3,1]$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186184072 1074 "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-6 & \\le -4x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-3 & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=x^2+2x-3$ lies below the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=1$ and $x=-3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/0f/0fe5ab0d38cea340e2227157bf1d434a0fccbab153289db52106857530fbc4dc.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving\n\n$$\nx^2+2x-3 \\le 0,\n$$\n\nwe look for the part of the parabola that is below the $x$-axis.  \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $-3 \\le x \\le 1.$  \n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x \\in [-3,1].$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186184073 1071 "Find the first $3$ terms in ascending powers of $a$ of the binomial expansion of $(1+3a)^5$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186184073 1071 "Find the first $3$ terms in ascending powers of $a$ of the binomial expansion of $(1+3a)^5.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186184073 1074 "The first $3$ terms of the binomial expansion for ${(1 + x)^n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n{(1 + x)^n} = 1 + nx + \\frac{{n(n - 1)}}{{2!}}{x^2}  +  \\cdots .\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=3a$ and $n=5$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+3a)}^{5} & =1+5(3a)+\\frac{5(5-1)}{2!}{(3a)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+5(3a)+\\frac{5(4)}{2}(9{a}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =1+15a+90{a}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186184073 1074 "The first $3$ terms of the binomial expansion for ${(1 + x)^n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n{(1 + x)^n} = 1 + nx + \\frac{{n(n - 1)}}{{2!}}{x^2} + \\cdots\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=3a$ and $n=5$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 + 3a)^{5} &= 1 + 5(3a) + (5(5 - 1))/(2!)(3a)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + 5(3a) + (5(4))/(2)(9a^{2}) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + 15a + 90a^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186184074 1071 "Find the solution of $x^2-2x-8 \\geq 0$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186184074 1071 "Find the solution of $x^2-2x-8 \\geq 0.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186184074 1074 "To solve the inequality\n\n$$\nx^2-2x-8 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola\n$y=x^2-2x-8$\nlies *above* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 8 &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 2)(x - 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-2$ and $x=4$, and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/6e/6e9eeff6caaad8efe92d4726c1f04175ed3023ea99b7990436597233ef2910df.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *above* the axis or touches this axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is\n\n$$\nx \\leq -2\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nx \\geq 4\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186184074 1074 "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\nx^2-2x-8 \\geq 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=x^2-2x-8\n$$\n\nlies *above* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-2$ and $x=4,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/6e/6e9eeff6caaad8efe92d4726c1f04175ed3023ea99b7990436597233ef2910df.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *above* the axis or touches this axis. \n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x \\leq -2$ or $x \\geq 4.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186184076 1071 "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $\\mathcal{B}= \\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, find $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186184076 1071 "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $\\mathcal{B}= \\{\\mathbf{b_1}, \\mathbf{b_2}, \\mathbf{b_3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, find $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186184076 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5$, and since $\\mathcal{B}$ is formed by $3$ vectors,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) = 3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nrank(A) + nullity(A) &= n \\\\\n3 + nullity(A) &= 5 \\\\\nnullity(A) &= 5 - 3 \\\\\ndim(Null(A)) &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186184076 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=5,$ and since $\\mathcal{B}$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186184077 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and  ![](images/ba/ba5c9db77780e1298423e58aaf6f9a54968db34643cfe98bfce96231ab6d3afc.png) \n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A)=1$\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n\n- $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186184077 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n1. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=1$\n2. $A$ is invertible\n3. $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186184077 1074 "Since $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n![](images/db/db1215d898d9a9af40907393d15bf2a03d1f709d857925e9756cfe15db2f9a07.png)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that \n![](images/59/5998f460a1889d2d938c3d530ef86c37f4a76f746ce0885e7892407aa7a2b02f.png) \nwhich means that $\\textrm{nullity}(A) =2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n = 3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(A) = 2 \\neq 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ cannot be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 1,$ we have that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $1$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186184077 1074 "Since $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nCol(A) = R^{3}; dim(Col(A)) = 3 = rank(A); Null(A) = {0}; dim(Null(A)) = 0 = nullity(A)\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhich means that $\\textrm{nullity}(A) =2$.Now, for the matrix $A$, the number of columns is $n = 3$, so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n$rank(A) + nullity(A)|= n; rank(A) + 2|= 3; rank(A)|= 3 - 2; = 1$.\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(A) = 2 \\neq 0$, then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ cannot be an invertible matrix.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 1$, we have that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $1$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186198234 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{x^2}{24} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} +\\dfrac{z^2}{6} = 1$ and the line\n${x = 4t; y = 8t,; z = 2t$\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186198234 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{x^2}{24} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} +\\dfrac{z^2}{6} = 1$ and the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=4t \\\\ y=8t, \\\\ z=2t\\end{aligned}$ \nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186198234 1074 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{24} + \\frac{y^{2}}{24} + \\frac{z^{2}}{6} &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} + y^{2} + 4z^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n(4t)^{2} + (8t)^{2} + 4(2t)^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n16t^{2} + 64t^{2} + 16t^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n96t^{2} &= 24 \\\\\nt^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution\n\n$$\nt = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\dfrac 1 2$, we have\n${x = 4(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-2; y = 8(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-4; z = 2(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-1$,\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,-4,-1\\right)$.\n- For $t=\\dfrac 1 2$, we have\n${x = 4(\\frac{1}{2}) = 2; y = 8(\\frac{1}{2}) = 4; z = 2(\\frac{1}{2}) = 1$,\nso the second point is $\\left(2,4,1\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-1)$ and $(2,4,1)$." 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186198234 1074 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =24 \\\\ (4t)^{2}+(8t)^{2}+4(2t)^{2} & =24 \\\\ 16{t}^{2}+64{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2} & =24 \\\\ 96{t}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\dfrac 1 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4(-\\frac{1}{2})=-2 \\\\ y=8(-\\frac{1}{2})=-4 \\\\ z=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,-4,-1\\right).$\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac 1 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4(\\frac{1}{2})=2 \\\\ y=8(\\frac{1}{2})=4 \\\\ z=2(\\frac{1}{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $\\left(2,4,1\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-1)$ and $(2,4,1).$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true] [17592186198235 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x$. First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx - z = 3⟹x = z + 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(((z + 3) + 1)^{2})/(16) + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n((z + 4)^{2})/(16) + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{z^{2} + 8z + 16}{16} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{2z^{2} + 8z + 16}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2} + 4z + 8}{8} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2} + 4z + 8}{8} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z^{2} + 4z + 4) + 4)/(8) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2} + 4)/(8) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(8) + \\frac{1}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(8) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{2}+\\dfrac{(z+2)^2}{4} = 1\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 false] [17592186198235 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z=3\\,⟹\\,x=z+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{((z+3)+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(z+4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}+8z+16}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{2{z}^{2}+8z+16}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}+4z+8}{8} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}+4z+8}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{({z}^{2}+4z+4)+4}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}+4}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\dfrac{y^2}{2}+\\dfrac{(z+2)^2}{4} = 1.$" 13194139533388 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"e-4769" 17592186119612 "e-477" 17592186096473 "e-4770" 17592186119613 "e-4771" 17592186119614 "e-4772" 17592186119615 "e-4775" 17592186119616 "e-4777" 17592186119617 "e-478" 17592186096474 "e-4782" 17592186119618